var e=JSON.parse(`[[901,"self in Python is a reference to the current instance of the class (the object) rather than to the class itself. It is used in instance methods to access the attributes and the other methods of that specific object. Without self a method would not be able to distinguish which object exactly it is working with. It is a convention rather than a keyword of the language, but breaking it will lead to errors.","What is self in Python?\\nIn Python \`self\` is a reference to a specific instance of a class (an object) rather than to the class itself. When you create an object of a class, Python automatically passes that object as the first argument into the instance methods. That lets a method work with the data of precisely that object rather than with the class's common data.\\nHow does it work?\\nLet us look at a simple example:\\n\\nHere \`self\` in the \`bark\` method points to the \`my_dog\` object, so \`self.name\` accesses the \`name\` attribute of precisely that object. If \`self\` referred to the class, then \`self.name\` would be common to every dog, which is incorrect.\\nWhy does that matter?\\nAn understanding of \`self\` is critical for working with object-oriented programming in Python. Without it, it is impossible to create methods that interact with the data of a specific object. It is the foundation of encapsulation and polymorphism.\\nConclusion\\n\`self\` always refers to an instance of a class rather than to the class itself. It is a fundamental concept for writing correct, readable classes in Python.","class Dog:\\n    def __init__(self, name):\\n        self.name = name  # self is the specific Dog object\\n\\n    def bark(self):\\n        print(f\\"{self.name} says woof!\\")\\n\\nmy_dog = Dog(\\"Rex\\")\\nmy_dog.bark()  # Output: Rex says woof!"],[902,"Multithreading is worth using for tasks that require parallel execution and can be divided into independent threads. It is especially useful for I/O-bound operations, where the threads wait for input-output. For CPU-bound tasks in Python, however, it is better to use multiprocessing because of the GIL. It matters to take the synchronisation of access to shared data into account, in order to avoid races.","When is it worth using multithreading?\\nMultithreading is the technique by which one process carries out several threads at the same time. It is useful for speeding up programs that spend a lot of time waiting for external resources, such as the reading of files, network requests or work with databases. In such cases threads let you avoid blocking the execution of other tasks.\\nThe main scenarios of application\\n\\n- I/O-bound tasks: input-output operations, where threads can wait for a response from the disk or the network while other threads work.\\n- The parallel processing of data: if the data can be divided into independent parts, each is processed in its own thread.\\n- User interfaces: so as not to block the UI while long operations are carried out.\\nAn example in Python\\n\\nThe limitations\\nIn Python, because of the Global Interpreter Lock (GIL), only one thread can carry out bytecode at any moment in time. For CPU-bound tasks (computations, for example) multithreading will therefore give no gain in performance — it is better to use multiprocessing. In other languages, such as Java or C++, there is no GIL, and multithreading is effective for any task.\\nConclusion\\nMultithreading is worth applying for I/O-bound operations, where responsiveness and parallel waiting matter. For CPU-bound tasks in Python choose multiprocessing, and in languages without a GIL use threads with confidence.","import threading\\nimport time\\n\\ndef fetch_data(url):\\n    print(f\\"Fetching {url}\\")\\n    time.sleep(2)  # an imitation of a delay\\n    print(f\\"Done {url}\\")\\n\\nurls = [\\"url1\\", \\"url2\\", \\"url3\\"]\\nthreads = []\\nfor url in urls:\\n    t = threading.Thread(target=fetch_data, args=(url,))\\n    threads.append(t)\\n    t.start()\\n\\nfor t in threads:\\n    t.join()\\nprint(\\"All done\\")"],[903,"No, not always. In the average case the search is carried out in O(1), but in the worst case, with a large number of collisions, the complexity can worsen to O(n). That happens when every key falls into one bucket — because of a poor hash function or an unfortunate size of table, for example.","An introduction\\nA hash table is a data structure that ensures fast access to elements by key. In ideal conditions the search, insertion and deletion are carried out in constant time O(1). In practice, however, that is not always the case.\\nThe factors affecting performance\\nThe main problem is collisions, where two different keys give the same hash. To resolve them, methods such as chaining or open addressing are used. With chaining, every bucket holds a linked list of elements. If there are many collisions, the length of the list grows and the search in it becomes linear O(n).\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nIn this example, if every key falls into one bucket, the \`get\` method will go through the whole list, which will give O(n).\\nHow to avoid the worsening\\n\\n- Use a good hash function, distributing the keys evenly.\\n- Maintain a low load factor and increase the size of the table where necessary (rehashing).\\n- In modern languages (Java, Python, for example) hash tables are balanced automatically.\\nConclusion\\nHash tables give O(1) on average, but they do not guarantee it in the worst case. For performance-critical applications it is worth taking the possibility of collisions into account and choosing suitable data structures or settings.","// A simple hash table with chaining\\nclass HashTable {\\n  constructor(size) {\\n    this.table = new Array(size);\\n  }\\n\\n  hash(key) {\\n    let hash = 0;\\n    for (let i = 0; i < key.length; i++) {\\n      hash += key.charCodeAt(i);\\n    }\\n    return hash % this.table.length;\\n  }\\n\\n  set(key, value) {\\n    const index = this.hash(key);\\n    if (!this.table[index]) {\\n      this.table[index] = [];\\n    }\\n    // We add or update the element\\n    for (let pair of this.table[index]) {\\n      if (pair[0] === key) {\\n        pair[1] = value;\\n        return;\\n      }\\n    }\\n    this.table[index].push([key, value]);\\n  }\\n\\n  get(key) {\\n    const index = this.hash(key);\\n    const bucket = this.table[index];\\n    if (bucket) {\\n      for (let pair of bucket) {\\n        if (pair[0] === key) {\\n          return pair[1];\\n        }\\n      }\\n    }\\n    return undefined;\\n  }\\n}"],[904,"A linked list consists of nodes, each of which holds the data and a reference to the next node. The built-in list (a dynamic array) holds the elements in a continuous block of memory. Insertion and deletion in the middle of a list are faster in a linked list, while access by index is faster in an array.","The main differences between a linked list and the built-in list\\nA linked list and the built-in list (a dynamic array) are two fundamental data structures, which organise the holding of and the access to elements differently. An understanding of their differences helps you choose the right structure for a specific task.\\nThe structure and the memory\\nThe built-in list holds the elements in a continuous block of memory, which ensures fast access by index in O(1). A linked list consists of separate nodes, each of which contains the data and a reference to the next node. The nodes can be scattered through the memory, which increases the overheads of holding the references.\\nThe performance of the operations\\n\\n- Access by index: list — O(1), a linked list — O(n) (you have to go from the beginning).\\n- Insertion/deletion at the beginning: list — O(n) (the shifting of the elements), a linked list — O(1).\\n- Insertion/deletion at the end: list — O(1) amortised, a linked list — O(1) with a tail pointer.\\n- Insertion/deletion in the middle: list — O(n), a linked list — O(1) once the position has been found.\\nAn example of code in Python\\n\\nWhen to use what\\nThe built-in list is ideal when frequent access by index or iteration with caching is needed. A linked list suits scenarios with frequent insertions and deletions at the beginning or in the middle better, especially when the size of the data changes dynamically.\\nConclusion: The choice between a linked list and the built-in list depends on the predominant operations: if fast access by index matters, use a list; if insertions/deletions are frequent, a linked list.","# The built-in list\\narr = [1, 2, 3]\\narr.insert(0, 0)  # O(n) — the shifting of every element\\nprint(arr[1])      # O(1) — fast access\\n\\n# A simple implementation of a singly linked list\\nclass Node:\\n    def __init__(self, data):\\n        self.data = data\\n        self.next = None\\n\\nclass LinkedList:\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        self.head = None\\n\\n    def prepend(self, data):\\n        new_node = Node(data)\\n        new_node.next = self.head\\n        self.head = new_node  # O(1) — insertion at the beginning\\n\\n    def get(self, index):\\n        current = self.head\\n        for _ in range(index):\\n            if not current:\\n                raise IndexError\\n            current = current.next\\n        return current.data  # O(n) — sequential access"],[905,"Iterating over a list has the complexity O(N) because every element is handled exactly once. If there are N elements in the list, N operations will be required. The execution time grows in direct proportion to the size of the input data, which is what is denoted as O(N).","What O(N) means in the context of iterating over a list\\nWhen we say that iterating over a list has the complexity O(N), we mean that the number of operations necessary to carry out the algorithm depends linearly on the size of the input data. It is one of the basic concepts of the analysis of algorithms, which helps you assess the performance of code.\\nWhy exactly a linear dependence\\nWhen iterating over a list we access every element in sequence exactly once. Regardless of whether we use a for loop, a while loop or recursion, every element has to be visited. If there are 10 elements in the list, 10 steps will be required; if 1000, 1000 steps. This direct proportionality is what O(N) is.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nHow that is applied in practice\\nAn understanding of O(N) helps developers choose effective algorithms. If you have to find an element in an unsorted list, for example, a linear search will have the complexity O(N). For sorted data you can use a binary search with O(log N), which is substantially faster with large volumes.\\nConclusion\\nThe complexity O(N) is a basic characteristic of algorithms where the execution time grows linearly with the size of the data. It matters to take it into account when working with large arrays, in order to avoid unjustified delays in performance.","const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\\nfor (let i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {\\n  console.log(numbers[i]); // an O(1) operation per element\\n}\\n// O(N) operations in all, where N = 5"],[906,"Data structures with a constant access time of O(1) let you carry out operations in a fixed time regardless of the size of the data. They include arrays (access by index), hash tables (insertion, deletion, search in the average case) and the stack/queue (the addition and removal of elements).","What is O(1) and why does it matter?\\nO(1) denotes constant time complexity — the execution time of an operation does not depend on the number of elements in the data structure. It is the ideal measure of performance, which people aim for when designing algorithms.\\nThe main structures with O(1)\\n\\n- An array: access to an element by index — O(1). \`arr[5]\`, for example, returns the value at once, since the address is computed as \`base_address + index * size_of_element\`.\\n- A hash table: insertion, deletion and search in the average case — O(1). The key is converted into an index through a hash function, which lets you find the cell needed quickly.\\n- A stack and a queue: the addition and removal of elements from one end (a stack) or from different ends (a queue) — O(1).\\nExamples of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nStructures with O(1) are critical for tasks where the speed of processing large volumes of data matters. They are worth applying when implementing caches, fast search systems and operations with a fixed response time.","// An array: access by index O(1)\\nconst arr = [10, 20, 30];\\nconsole.log(arr[1]); // 20\\n\\n// A hash table (an object): search O(1)\\nconst map = { 'a': 1, 'b': 2 };\\nconsole.log(map['a']); // 1\\n\\n// A stack: push/pop O(1)\\nconst stack = [];\\nstack.push(1);\\nstack.pop();"],[907,"O(N!) is the notation for the factorial time complexity of an algorithm. It means that the execution time grows as the product of every number from 1 to N. Such a complexity is met in algorithms that go through every permutation — in the travelling-salesman problem, for example. In practice algorithms with O(N!) become unfeasible already at N > 10-12.","What is O(N!)?\\nO(N!) is the notation for factorial time complexity in Big O notation. It describes algorithms whose execution time grows in proportion to the factorial of the size of the input data N. The factorial N! is the product of every natural number from 1 to N: N! = 1 * 2 * 3 * ... * N.\\nWhere is it met?\\nSuch a complexity is typical of algorithms that go through every possible permutation of the elements. The classic example is the solution of the travelling-salesman problem by the method of exhaustive search, where you have to find the shortest route through N cities, visiting each exactly once.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nThe practical limitations\\n\\n- At N=10: 10! = 3,628,800 operations — still feasible\\n- At N=20: 20! ≈ 2.4 * 10^18 — practically unfeasible\\n- At N=100: the number of operations exceeds the number of atoms in the Universe\\nConclusion: algorithms with factorial complexity should be avoided in real projects. If a task requires going through permutations, it is necessary to look for approximate solutions or to use heuristics.","function generatePermutations(arr) {\\n  if (arr.length <= 1) return [arr];\\n  const result = [];\\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    const current = arr[i];\\n    const remaining = arr.slice(0, i).concat(arr.slice(i + 1));\\n    const perms = generatePermutations(remaining);\\n    for (let perm of perms) {\\n      result.push([current].concat(perm));\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return result;\\n}\\n// For an array of N elements there will be N! permutations\\nconsole.log(generatePermutations([1,2,3]).length); // 6 = 3!"],[908,"The main graph traversal algorithms are depth-first search (DFS) and breadth-first search (BFS). DFS uses a stack and goes deep, while BFS uses a queue and traverses level by level. They are applied to search for paths, check connectivity and for topological sorting.","The main graph traversal algorithms\\nThe traversal of a graph is the process of visiting every vertex of the graph in a particular order. The two fundamental algorithms are depth-first search (DFS) and breadth-first search (BFS). They differ in their strategy of traversal and in the data structures they use.\\nDepth-first search (DFS)\\nDFS uses a stack (explicitly or through recursion) and goes as deep as possible along every path before returning. It is applied for topological sorting and for the search for cycles and connected components.\\n\\nBreadth-first search (BFS)\\nBFS uses a queue and traverses the graph by levels: first every vertex at distance 1, then at 2 and so on. It is ideal for finding the shortest path in an unweighted graph.\\n\\nAdditional algorithms\\n\\n- Dijkstra's algorithm — for the shortest paths in a weighted graph with non-negative weights.\\n- The Bellman-Ford algorithm — for graphs with negative weights.\\n- Topological sorting — for directed acyclic graphs (DAGs).\\nThe choice of algorithm depends on the task: BFS for the shortest path in an unweighted graph, DFS for checking connectivity or searching for cycles.","def dfs(graph, start, visited=None):\\n    if visited is None:\\n        visited = set()\\n    visited.add(start)\\n    for neighbor in graph[start]:\\n        if neighbor not in visited:\\n            dfs(graph, neighbor, visited)\\n    return visited\\n\\nfrom collections import deque\\n\\ndef bfs(graph, start):\\n    visited = set([start])\\n    queue = deque([start])\\n    while queue:\\n        vertex = queue.popleft()\\n        for neighbor in graph[vertex]:\\n            if neighbor not in visited:\\n                visited.add(neighbor)\\n                queue.append(neighbor)\\n    return visited"],[909,"Nested loops have the complexity O(N²) because for every one of the N elements of the outer loop the inner loop is carried out N times. The total number of iterations is thus equal to N * N = N². That means that when N is doubled the execution time increases fourfold.","What is O(N²) and why do nested loops lead to such a complexity?\\nWhen we speak of the computational complexity of an algorithm, we assess how the number of operations grows as the size of the input data increases. O(N²) is quadratic complexity, in which the number of operations is proportional to the square of the size of the data.\\nNested loops are the classic example of such a situation. Imagine you have an array of N elements and you want to compare every element with every other. The outer loop goes over all N elements, and for every one of them the inner loop goes over N elements again. The result is N × N = N² operations.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nIf the array contains 10 elements, there will be 100 lines printed to the console. If 100, then 10,000 already. The growth is obvious.\\nWhere is that applied?\\n\\n- Bubble Sort — O(N²) in the worst case.\\n- The search for duplicates in an unsorted array without additional memory.\\n- Going through every pair in combinatorics tasks.\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of O(N²) helps you avoid ineffective solutions when working with large data. If nested loops can be replaced with more effective algorithms (using hash tables for O(N), for example), that is worth doing. Quadratic complexity is acceptable only for small arrays or when there are no other options.","function printPairs(arr) {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {      // the outer loop: N times\\n    for (let j = 0; j < arr.length; j++) {    // the inner loop: N times\\n      console.log(arr[i], arr[j]);            // N² operations in all\\n    }\\n  }\\n}"],[910,"O(log N) describes algorithms whose execution time grows logarithmically relative to the size of the input data. That means that when the data increases tenfold, the time increases only by a constant. The classic example is a binary search in a sorted array. Such algorithms are very effective for large volumes of data.","What is O(log N)?\\nO(log N) is the notation for the logarithmic complexity of an algorithm. It shows that the execution time of the algorithm grows in proportion to the logarithm of the size of the input data. In computer science the logarithm to base 2 is usually used, since algorithms often divide the data in half.\\nHow does it work?\\nImagine you have a sorted list of 1000 elements. A linear search (O(N)) will require 1000 steps in the worst case. A binary search (O(log N)) will require only about 10 steps (log2(1000) ≈ 10). At every step the algorithm discards half of the data, which is what gives the logarithmic growth.\\nAn example of code: a binary search\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- The search in sorted data structures (arrays, search trees).\\n- Algorithms on graphs (the search for the shortest path using a heap, for example).\\n- Work with databases (the indexes of B-trees).\\nConclusion\\nO(log N) is a very effective complexity, which is worth using when working with large volumes of data, when a fast search or insertion is required. It is substantially faster than linear (O(N)) and is the gold standard for many algorithms.","function binarySearch(arr, target) {\\n  let left = 0;\\n  let right = arr.length - 1;\\n\\n  while (left <= right) {\\n    const mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);\\n    if (arr[mid] === target) return mid;\\n    if (arr[mid] < target) left = mid + 1;\\n    else right = mid - 1;\\n  }\\n  return -1;\\n}"],[911,"In JavaScript you can create a custom event by means of the \`CustomEvent\` constructor. That event can be initiated by means of the \`dispatchEvent()\` method. Custom events are useful when you have to signal some action or change of state that is not connected with the browser's built-in events.","Custom events in JavaScript are created by means of the \`CustomEvent\` object. That lets developers create their own events and pass custom data through them. To initiate an event the \`dispatchEvent()\` method is used, which starts the event on the element given.\\nAn example of creating and initiating a custom event:\\n\\nIn this example a custom event with the name \`'myCustomEvent'\` is created, which passes additional data through the \`detail\` object. The event is handled on the element given by means of an ordinary event handler.\\nCustom events are useful for creating your own interaction between the components of an application, especially in complex interfaces where you have to signal actions that are not connected with the browser's built-in events (the switching of a state in an SPA, for example).","// We create the custom event \\nconst myEvent = new CustomEvent('myCustomEvent', {\\n  detail: { message: 'The custom event has been started' } \\n}); \\n// The element on which the event will be started \\nconst element = document.querySelector('.myElement'); \\n// We add the event handler \\nelement.addEventListener('myCustomEvent', function(event) {\\n  console.log(event.detail.message); // 'The custom event has been started' \\n}); \\n// We initiate the event \\nelement.dispatchEvent(myEvent);"],[912,"Combinatorial tasks are tasks in which you have to count the number of possible variants of choosing or arranging elements. They are based on the rules of combinatorics: permutations, arrangements and combinations. For example, how many ways there are of choosing 3 books out of 10, or how many different passwords can be composed from letters and digits.","What are combinatorial tasks?\\nCombinatorial tasks are a class of mathematical and algorithmic problems in which it is required to determine the number of ways of choosing, arranging or combining objects from a given set. They are widely applied in programming, probability theory, cryptography and optimisation.\\nThe main concepts\\n\\n- Permutations — the number of ways of ordering n different elements: n! (the factorial).\\n- Arrangements — the number of ways of choosing and ordering k elements out of n: A(n,k) = n! / (n-k)!.\\n- Combinations — the number of ways of choosing k elements out of n without taking the order into account: C(n,k) = n! / (k! * (n-k)!).\\nAn example of code in Python\\n\\nApplication in programming\\nCombinatorial tasks are met when generating passwords, analysing algorithms (the complexity of exhaustive search, for example), in tasks on graphs (the number of paths) and in testing (combinatorial coverage testing).\\nConclusion: Combinatorial tasks help you assess the number of variants in systems with discrete elements. An understanding of them is necessary for developing effective algorithms and analysing complexity.","import math\\n\\n# The number of ways of choosing 3 books out of 10 (combinations)\\nn = 10\\nk = 3\\ncombinations = math.comb(n, k)\\nprint(f\\"Combinations: {combinations}\\")  # 120\\n\\n# The number of ways of ordering 5 books (permutations)\\npermutations = math.factorial(5)\\nprint(f\\"Permutations: {permutations}\\")  # 120\\n\\n# Arrangements: choose and order 2 out of 4\\nfrom math import perm\\narrangements = perm(4, 2)\\nprint(f\\"Arrangements: {arrangements}\\")  # 12"],[913,"O(N²) is the notation for the time complexity of an algorithm that grows in proportion to the square of the size of the input data. That means that if the size of the data doubles, the execution time increases fourfold. Most often such a complexity arises with the use of nested loops, where every element is compared with every other. Bubble sort, for example, has the complexity O(N²).","What is O(N²)?\\nO(N²) is the notation for the time complexity of an algorithm that grows in proportion to the square of the size of the input data N. That means that when N doubles the execution time increases fourfold. Such a complexity is characteristic of algorithms where every element is handled in a pair with every other element.\\nExamples of algorithms with O(N²)\\n\\n- Bubble Sort\\n- Insertion Sort in the worst case\\n- The checking of every pair of elements in an array\\nAn example of code\\n\\nIn this example the outer loop is carried out N times, while the inner one is carried out N/2 times on average, which gives approximately N²/2 operations. The constants are discarded, so the complexity is O(N²).\\nWhen does that matter?\\nO(N²) is a slow complexity for large N. If N = 1000, about 1,000,000 operations will be required. For N = 10,000 — 100,000,000 operations already. Algorithms with O(N²) are therefore applied only for small sets of data or when other algorithms do not suit.\\nConclusion: O(N²) is quadratic complexity, which should be avoided for large data. Where possible, use algorithms with O(N log N) or O(N).","function bubbleSort(arr) {\\n  let n = arr.length;\\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\n    for (let j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {\\n      if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {\\n        // we swap them round\\n        [arr[j], arr[j + 1]] = [arr[j + 1], arr[j]];\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return arr;\\n}"],[914,"Depth-first traversal (DFS) is an algorithm that begins at the root vertex and goes as deep as possible along every path before returning. It uses a stack (explicitly or through recursion) to keep track of the vertices. DFS is applied to search for paths, check connectivity, for topological sorting and to solve puzzles.","What is depth-first traversal?\\nDepth-First Search (DFS) is an algorithm for traversing or searching in data structures such as graphs and trees. It begins at a chosen starting vertex and recursively or iteratively investigates every branch to the end before returning back. That contrasts with breadth-first traversal (BFS), which investigates every vertex at the current level before moving on to the next.\\nHow does DFS work?\\nThe algorithm uses a stack to remember the vertices that have to be visited. In the recursive implementation the stack is created automatically by the function calls. The main steps:\\n\\n- Mark the current vertex as visited.\\n- For every neighbour that has not yet been visited, call DFS recursively.\\n- When every neighbour has been handled, return to the previous vertex.\\nAn example of the implementation in JavaScript\\n\\nThe application of DFS\\nDFS is used in many tasks:\\n\\n- The search for a path in a maze or a graph.\\n- The checking of a graph's connectivity.\\n- Topological sorting (in directed acyclic graphs).\\n- The detection of cycles.\\n- The solution of puzzles, such as sudoku or the eight-queens problem.\\nConclusion\\nDFS is a fundamental algorithm for working with graphs, which is effective for the deep investigation of a structure. It is worth applying when you have to find every possible path or check the properties of a graph, especially if the graph is not too wide.","const graph = {\\n  A: ['B', 'C'],\\n  B: ['A', 'D', 'E'],\\n  C: ['A', 'F'],\\n  D: ['B'],\\n  E: ['B', 'F'],\\n  F: ['C', 'E']\\n};\\n\\nfunction dfs(graph, start, visited = new Set()) {\\n  visited.add(start);\\n  console.log(start);\\n  for (const neighbor of graph[start]) {\\n    if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {\\n      dfs(graph, neighbor, visited);\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\ndfs(graph, 'A'); // A, B, D, E, F, C"],[915,"The classic algorithms are time-proven methods of solving tasks, such as sorting, searching and the traversal of graphs. They are the foundation for building complex software systems. An understanding of these algorithms helps you write effective, optimised code.","The classic algorithms represent fundamental methods of handling data, which were developed and analysed decades ago. They cover a broad spectrum of tasks: from the simple sorting of numbers to complex operations with graphs and strings. A knowledge of these algorithms is necessary for any developer, since they lie at the basis of most modern software solutions.\\nThe main categories of classic algorithm\\n\\n- Sorting: bubble, quick, merge, insertion — used to put data in order.\\n- Searching: linear, binary — to find an element in a data structure.\\n- Graph algorithms: depth-first search (DFS), breadth-first search (BFS), Dijkstra's algorithm — for working with networks and connections.\\n- Dynamic programming: the solution of tasks by breaking them up into subtasks (the knapsack problem, for example).\\nAn example: a binary search\\nA binary search is an effective algorithm for finding an element in a sorted array. It works by dividing the array in half and comparing the value sought with the middle element.\\n\\nThis algorithm has logarithmic complexity O(log n), which makes it substantially faster than a linear search for large arrays.\\nApplication in practice\\nThe classic algorithms are used everywhere: in databases (sorting and indexing), in search engines (ranking), in routing (graph algorithms) and in machine learning (optimisation).\\nConclusion: The study of the classic algorithms develops algorithmic thinking and lets you build effective, scalable solutions. They are a mandatory tool in every developer's arsenal, especially when working with large volumes of data or in real-time systems.","function binarySearch(arr, target) {\\n  let left = 0;\\n  let right = arr.length - 1;\\n\\n  while (left <= right) {\\n    const mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);\\n    if (arr[mid] === target) return mid;\\n    if (arr[mid] < target) left = mid + 1;\\n    else right = mid - 1;\\n  }\\n  return -1;\\n}"],[916,"The time complexity of an algorithm is an assessment of how the execution time of the algorithm depends on the size of the input data. It is expressed by means of Big O notation — O(n) or O(log n), for example. That helps you compare the efficiency of different algorithms without a tie to specific hardware.","What is time complexity?\\nThe time complexity of an algorithm is a mathematical assessment of the number of operations the algorithm carries out depending on the size of the input data (n). It does not measure the real time in seconds but shows how quickly the execution time grows as n increases. It is a key tool for choosing the optimal algorithm.\\nThe main kinds of complexity\\n\\n- O(1) — constant: the time does not depend on n (access to an element of an array by index, for example).\\n- O(log n) — logarithmic: the time grows slowly (a binary search, for example).\\n- O(n) — linear: the time is proportional to n (a search in an unsorted array, for example).\\n- O(n log n) — often met in effective sorts (quicksort, for example).\\n- O(n²) — quadratic: the time grows quickly (bubble sort, for example).\\nAn example of code\\n\\nHow do you apply it?\\nWhen choosing an algorithm for a task it matters to take the expected size of the data into account. For small arrays (n < 100) the difference between O(n²) and O(n log n) may be unnoticeable, but for large data (n > 10,000) it becomes critical. Always aim for the minimum possible complexity, but remember the trade-off with memory.\\nConclusion: An understanding of time complexity helps you write effective code and avoid bottlenecks in performance, especially when working with large volumes of data.","// O(1) — constant complexity\\nfunction getFirstElement(arr) {\\n  return arr[0];\\n}\\n\\n// O(n) — linear complexity\\nfunction findElement(arr, target) {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    if (arr[i] === target) return i;\\n  }\\n  return -1;\\n}\\n\\n// O(n²) — quadratic complexity\\nfunction bubbleSort(arr) {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    for (let j = 0; j < arr.length - 1; j++) {\\n      if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {\\n        [arr[j], arr[j + 1]] = [arr[j + 1], arr[j]];\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return arr;\\n}"],[917,"A binary search works with a sorted array. At every step the algorithm compares the value sought with the middle element. If the value is less than the middle one, the search continues in the left half; otherwise in the right. The search range is thus halved each time, until the element is found or the range becomes empty.","How the search range is reduced in a binary search\\nA binary search is an effective algorithm for finding an element in a sorted array. Its key idea consists in dividing the current search range in half at every step, discarding the half in which the element sought certainly is not. That lets you achieve logarithmic complexity O(log n).\\nThe principle of its working\\nThe algorithm begins with the full range from the left index (low) to the right one (high). The middle index (mid) is computed. If the value at mid is equal to the one sought, the search is finished. If the value sought is less than the value at mid, then every element to the right of mid is greater than the one sought, so the range narrows to [low, mid-1]. If it is greater, the range becomes [mid+1, high].\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nA visualisation of the reduction of the range\\nLet us look at the array [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13] and the search for the number 7:\\n\\n- Step 1: low=0, high=6, mid=3 (the value 7) — found.\\n- If we had been looking for 5: low=0, high=6, mid=3 (7 > 5) → high=2; then low=0, high=2, mid=1 (3 < 5) → low=2; low=2, high=2, mid=2 (5) — found.\\nEvery step halves the number of elements under consideration. For an array of 8 elements no more than 4 steps will be required, for 16 no more than 5, and so on.\\nConclusion\\nA binary search is applied everywhere the data is sorted and a fast search is required: in databases, search engines and when working with large sets of data. Its efficiency makes it an indispensable tool in a developer's arsenal.","function binarySearch(arr, target) {\\n  let low = 0;\\n  let high = arr.length - 1;\\n\\n  while (low <= high) {\\n    const mid = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);\\n    if (arr[mid] === target) return mid;\\n    if (arr[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;\\n    else high = mid - 1;\\n  }\\n  return -1;\\n}"],[918,"An iterator in JavaScript has to implement the next() method, which returns an object with two properties: value (the current value) and done (the completion flag). An object can also be iterable if it has a Symbol.iterator method returning an iterator. That lets you use it in for...of loops, in the spread operator and in destructuring.","What is an iterator and which methods does it have to implement?\\nAn iterator is an object that provides sequential access to the elements of a collection. In JavaScript an iterator has to implement the \`next()\` method, which returns an object with two fields: \`value\` (the current value) and \`done\` (a boolean value stating whether the iteration has finished).\\nThe iteration protocol\\nThe iteration protocol consists of two parts: the iterable object (which has a \`Symbol.iterator\` method) and the iterator itself (returned by that method). The iterator has to have a \`next()\` method, which is called at every step of the loop.\\nAn example of the implementation\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nIterators are used in \`for...of\` loops, in the spread operator (\`[...iterable]\`), in the destructuring of arrays, and also in the \`Array.from()\` and \`Promise.all()\` methods. They lie at the basis of the working of many built-in objects, such as \`Array\`, \`Map\`, \`Set\` and \`String\`.\\nConclusion\\nIterators are a fundamental mechanism for working with sequences of data in JavaScript. An understanding of them is necessary for creating your own iterable structures and for using the language's built-in capabilities effectively.","const myIterable = {\\n  [Symbol.iterator]() {\\n    let step = 0;\\n    return {\\n      next() {\\n        step++;\\n        if (step <= 3) {\\n          return { value: step, done: false };\\n        }\\n        return { value: undefined, done: true };\\n      }\\n    };\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nfor (const num of myIterable) {\\n  console.log(num); // 1, 2, 3\\n}"],[919,"The distance between the indices of elements is computed as the absolute difference of their positions. For the array [a, b, c], for example, the distance between the indices 0 and 2 is equal to 2. That is used in tasks of searching for the nearest elements or of analysing data.","The main concept\\nThe distance between the indices of elements in an array is the number of steps between their positions. It is computed as the modulus of the difference of the indices: \`|i - j|\`. It is a fundamental operation in algorithms such as the search for the minimum distance between identical elements or the analysis of time series.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nApplication\\n\\n- The search for the nearest duplicates in an array.\\n- The analysis of distances in graphs represented by lists.\\n- The optimisation of queries to databases.\\nConclusion: The computation of the distance between indices is a simple but important operation, used in algorithms for handling data and for optimisation.","function distanceBetweenIndices(arr, elem1, elem2) {\\n  const i1 = arr.indexOf(elem1);\\n  const i2 = arr.indexOf(elem2);\\n  if (i1 === -1 || i2 === -1) return -1;\\n  return Math.abs(i1 - i2);\\n}\\n\\nconst arr = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];\\nconsole.log(distanceBetweenIndices(arr, 10, 40)); // 3"],[920,"return is used when the result of a function's work has to be passed into another part of the program for further handling. print prints data to the screen but does not return it. If a function has to give out a value for computations or logic, use return. If you simply have to show information to the user, print.","The difference between return and print is the difference between passing data and printing to the screen. return ends the execution of a function and sends a value back into the calling code. print, for its part, simply prints text to the console but returns no value (it returns None).\\nWhen to use return\\nreturn is necessary when the result of a function is needed for further computations, for assignment to a variable or for passing into another function. Without return a function cannot take part in a program's logic as a source of data.\\n\\nWhen to use print\\nprint is used for debugging, for logging or for printing information to the user. It is not intended for passing data into the code.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse return when a function has to give out a value for further handling. Use print only to print information, not to pass data. That is the foundation of writing clean, reusable code.","def add(a, b):\\n    return a + b\\n\\nresult = add(3, 5)  # result = 8\\nprint(result * 2)   # 16\\n\\ndef show_sum(a, b):\\n    print(a + b)  # prints 8, but returns None\\n\\nresult = show_sum(3, 5)  # result = None\\nprint(result * 2)  # An error: NoneType"],[921,"To check whether a variable has been initialised, you can use the typeof operator, which returns 'undefined' for uninitialised variables. You can also compare with undefined, but that will raise an error if the variable has not been declared. To check for null or undefined, strict comparison or the ?? operator is often used.","Checking the initialisation of a variable\\nIn JavaScript a variable can be uninitialised if it has been declared but has no value (undefined), or if it has not been declared at all. The check for initialisation matters for preventing errors when accessing data.\\nThe main ways of checking\\n\\n- The typeof operator: it safely checks whether a variable has been declared, returning the string 'undefined' for undeclared or uninitialised variables.\\n- Comparison with undefined: it works only for declared variables; otherwise it raises a ReferenceError.\\n- The check for null: if a variable can be null, use strict comparison (===) or the nullish coalescing operator (??).\\nExamples of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse typeof for the safe checking of a variable's existence, especially in the global context. To check for null or undefined, apply the ?? operator or strict comparison, in order to avoid unexpected errors.","let a;\\nconsole.log(typeof a); // 'undefined'\\nconsole.log(a === undefined); // true\\n\\nvar b = null;\\nconsole.log(b == null); // true (a check for null and undefined)\\nconsole.log(b ?? 'default'); // 'default'\\n\\n// For an undeclared variable\\nconsole.log(typeof c); // 'undefined' (without an error)\\n// console.log(c === undefined); // ReferenceError"],[922,"The event loop is the mechanism in JavaScript that manages the execution of code, the handling of events and the carrying out of the tasks from the queue (the callbacks). It lets JavaScript be asynchronous by handling tasks when the main program has already finished. When an asynchronous operation is called, it is sent into the queue, and the event loop tracks and carries out the tasks as soon as the main thread is free.","The event loop in JavaScript is responsible for the asynchronous execution of tasks. JavaScript is single-threaded, which means that the code is carried out in turn in a single thread. To work with asynchronous operations (such as requests to a server or timers), however, the event model is used.\\nWhen an asynchronous operation is called, it is passed to outside APIs (the Web API in the browser, for example), and as soon as the operation has finished, the result is placed in the callback queue. The event loop watches that queue and, when the main call stack is empty, it carries out the tasks from the queue.\\nAn example using \`setTimeout()\`:\\n\\nThe result will be:\\n\\nEven if \`setTimeout\` is set to 0 milliseconds, its callback will fall into the queue and will be carried out only after the main code has finished.\\nThe event loop gives JavaScript the ability to handle asynchronous tasks and lets you build dynamic, interactive web applications.","console.log('The first call');\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log('The asynchronous call'); \\n}, 0); \\nconsole.log('The second call');\\n\\n// The first call \\n// The second call \\n// The asynchronous call"],[923,"If a string consists only of identical characters, then when solving tasks connected with the search for unique characters or the counting of frequency, the result may be trivial. When searching for the first non-repeating character, for example, there will be no answer. It matters to provide for such a check, in order to avoid errors or incorrect results.","The handling of a string with identical characters\\nWhen a string consists only of identical characters, many algorithms that work with strings can give unexpected results. When searching for the first non-repeating character or when checking for an anagram, for example. It matters to handle this case explicitly, so that the program does not crash and gives a correct answer.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nWhere this is applied\\n\\n- The validation of passwords or of the data entered.\\n- The analysis of text — the search for rare characters, for example.\\n- The solution of interview tasks, where the boundary cases have to be taken into account.\\nConclusion: The handling of strings with identical characters is an important boundary case, which it is necessary to take into account when developing algorithms for working with strings, in order to ensure the correctness and the resilience of a program.","function firstUniqueChar(str) {\\n  const charCount = {};\\n  for (let char of str) {\\n    charCount[char] = (charCount[char] || 0) + 1;\\n  }\\n  for (let char of str) {\\n    if (charCount[char] === 1) {\\n      return char;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return null; // Every character is identical, or the string is empty\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(firstUniqueChar('aaaa')); // null\\nconsole.log(firstUniqueChar('abca')); // 'b'"],[924,"You can use the includes() method for every character, or a regular expression with a character class. For example, str.includes('a') && str.includes('b') or /[ab]/.test(str). The second way is shorter, but it checks for the presence of at least one of the characters.","Checking for the presence of two different characters in a string\\nTo check for the presence of two different characters in a string in JavaScript you can use several approaches. The simplest is the \`includes()\` method, which checks every character separately. Alternatively you can apply a regular expression with a character class, but it checks for the presence of at least one of the characters stated rather than of both at once.\\nAn example with includes()\\n\\nAn example with a regular expression\\n\\nIf you have to check for the presence of both characters, a regular expression does not suit directly. In such a case it is better to use \`includes()\` or to combine the methods. For more complex checks (the order of the characters, for example) regular expressions with capturing groups are applied.\\nConclusion\\nUse \`includes()\` for the simple checking of the presence of several characters, and regular expressions when flexibility in the search for patterns or a check of the order of the characters is required.","const str = 'hello world';\\nconst hasA = str.includes('a');\\nconst hasB = str.includes('b');\\nif (hasA && hasB) {\\n  console.log('The string contains both a and b');\\n} else {\\n  console.log('The string does not contain both characters');\\n}\\n\\nconst str = 'hello world';\\nconst regex = /[ab]/;\\nif (regex.test(str)) {\\n  console.log('The string contains at least one of the characters a or b');\\n} else {\\n  console.log('The string contains neither a nor b');\\n}"],[925,"You have to go over the string, remembering the last position of every character. On meeting a repeating character we compute the difference between the current and the last position and update the minimum distance. At the end we return the minimum found.","The search for the shortest distance between repeating characters\\nThe task consists in finding the minimum distance between two identical characters in a string. The distance is usually measured as the difference of the indices (the absolute value). It is a classic string-handling task, which tests the ability to use hash tables or arrays effectively to track the positions of characters.\\nThe algorithm\\nWe can solve the task in a single pass over the string, using a dictionary to hold the last occurrence of every character. On meeting a character that has already been met, we compute the distance to its previous position and update the minimum value. If the character is met for the first time, we simply save its index.\\nAn example of code in JavaScript\\n\\nApplication\\nSimilar algorithms are used in tasks of data compression, the search for patterns and the analysis of text. For determining repeating substrings or assessing the diversity of the characters, for example.\\nConclusion: This approach is effective thanks to the single-pass algorithm using a hash table, which gives a time complexity of O(n) and memory of O(k), where k is the number of unique characters. Apply it when you have to find the minimum distance between identical elements in a sequence quickly.","function minDistanceBetweenRepeats(str) {\\n  const lastPos = {};\\n  let minDist = Infinity;\\n  for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++) {\\n    const char = str[i];\\n    if (char in lastPos) {\\n      const dist = i - lastPos[char];\\n      if (dist < minDist) minDist = dist;\\n    }\\n    lastPos[char] = i;\\n  }\\n  return minDist === Infinity ? -1 : minDist;\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(minDistanceBetweenRepeats(\\"abca\\")); // 3 (a at 0 and 3)\\nconsole.log(minDistanceBetweenRepeats(\\"abcabc\\")); // 3 (a at 0 and 3)"],[926,"To handle absent elements, use checks for null/undefined, the optional chaining operator (?.) or the try...catch construction. Optional chaining lets you safely access nested properties, returning undefined instead of an error. That makes the code more readable and prevents the application from crashing.","The handling of absent elements in JavaScript\\nIn JavaScript absent elements can be represented as null, undefined or simply non-existent properties of an object. The handling of such situations is critical for an application's stability, especially when working with data from external sources (APIs, user input).\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- The check for null/undefined: Use conditional statements to check a value before accessing its properties.\\n- Optional chaining (?.) Lets you safely access nested properties, returning undefined if an intermediate value is equal to null or undefined.\\n- The nullish coalescing operator (??) Returns the right operand if the left is equal to null or undefined, which is useful for setting default values.\\n- try...catch Used to handle exceptions when code can throw an error (when parsing JSON, for example).\\nExamples of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse optional chaining and the nullish coalescing operator for the concise handling of absent data in modern projects. For more complex scenarios (work with external APIs, for example) apply try...catch, in order to guarantee that the application does not crash because of unexpected errors.","// An example with optional chaining\\nconst user = { name: 'Alice', address: null };\\nconst city = user.address?.city; // undefined, without an error\\n\\n// An example with a check\\nif (user.address && user.address.city) {\\n  console.log(user.address.city);\\n} else {\\n  console.log('The address has not been stated');\\n}\\n\\n// An example with try...catch\\nlet data;\\ntry {\\n  data = JSON.parse('{invalid json}');\\n} catch (error) {\\n  console.error('An error of parsing:', error.message);\\n  data = {}; // the default value\\n}\\n\\n// An example with the nullish coalescing operator\\nconst timeout = config.timeout ?? 3000; // 3000, if config.timeout is null/undefined"],[927,"In a sorted array the elements are arranged in ascending order, so the difference of the indices directly corresponds to the difference of the values. To find the minimum difference between neighbouring elements it is enough to compute the difference of their indices, since the values grow monotonically. That lets you avoid complex computations and simply go over the array, comparing neighbouring elements.","Why does the modulus of the difference of the indices work for finding the minimum distance?\\nIn a sorted array the elements are arranged in ascending (or descending) order. That means the difference between the values of two elements is directly proportional to the difference of their indices, if the array consists of consecutive whole numbers or has an even step. In the general case, however, when the array is sorted but the values can be arbitrary, the minimum difference between any two elements will always be achieved among neighbouring elements. To find the minimum distance it is therefore enough to compute the difference between neighbouring elements, which is equivalent to the difference of their indices if the array contains consecutive numbers.\\nAn example\\nLet us look at the sorted array: [1, 3, 6, 10]. The difference between the neighbouring elements: 2, 3, 4. The minimum difference is 2 (between 1 and 3). If the array had been unsorted, we would have had to compare every pair, which would have taken O(n^2). Thanks to the sorting, we can find the minimum difference in O(n) by simply going over the array and computing the difference between the neighbouring elements.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe use of the modulus of the difference of the indices to find the minimum distance is applicable only in the context of sorted arrays with consecutive values. In the general case, for arbitrary sorted data, the minimum difference is found among the neighbouring elements, which simplifies the algorithm to linear complexity.","function minDifference(arr) {\\n  arr.sort((a, b) => a - b);\\n  let minDiff = Infinity;\\n  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    minDiff = Math.min(minDiff, arr[i] - arr[i-1]);\\n  }\\n  return minDiff;\\n}\\nconsole.log(minDifference([10, 3, 6, 1])); // 2"],[928,"To test an algorithm you have to determine a set of cases: ordinary input data, boundary values, empty or incorrect data. Use unit tests to automate the checking. It matters to cover both the expected scenarios and the extreme cases, in order to be sure the algorithm works correctly.","The main principles of testing algorithms\\nThe testing of an algorithm is the process of checking its correctness on various sets of input data. The main aim is to be sure the algorithm works correctly not only in typical situations but also in boundary ones, and with incorrect data. For that, several categories of test case are singled out.\\nThe categories of test case\\n\\n- Ordinary cases — the typical input data that the algorithm has to handle most often. For the sorting of an array, for example — an array of several random numbers.\\n- Boundary cases — minimum, maximum or empty values. An empty array, an array of one element, an array with identical elements, for example.\\n- Incorrect data — data that does not conform to the format expected (null, negative numbers if the algorithm expects only positive ones, for example).\\n- Performance — tests on large volumes of data to check the execution time and the use of memory.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nTools for automation\\nFor serious projects, frameworks for unit testing are used, such as Jest (JavaScript), PyTest (Python) or JUnit (Java). They let you structure the tests, run them automatically and obtain reports on the coverage of the code.\\nConclusion: The testing of algorithms on various cases is a mandatory practice for ensuring the reliability of code. It is especially important to cover the boundary cases, since it is precisely in them that errors most often arise. Use automated tests for a regular check.","function sumArray(arr) {\\n  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) throw new Error('Input must be an array');\\n  return arr.reduce((acc, val) => acc + val, 0);\\n}\\n\\n// The tests\\nconsole.assert(sumArray([1, 2, 3]) === 6, 'An ordinary case');\\nconsole.assert(sumArray([]) === 0, 'An empty array');\\nconsole.assert(sumArray([5]) === 5, 'One element');\\nconsole.assert(sumArray([-1, 0, 1]) === 0, 'Negative numbers');\\ntry {\\n  sumArray(null);\\n} catch (e) {\\n  console.assert(e.message === 'Input must be an array', 'Incorrect data');\\n}"],[929,"Algorithms can fail on edge tests because of boundary conditions that have not been taken into account, such as empty arrays, minimum or maximum values, zero lengths or negative numbers. Often that is connected with errors in the logic of the comparisons, in the indexing or in the handling of exceptional situations. To prevent such problems it is necessary to check and handle every possible edge case explicitly at the design and testing stage.","Why algorithms fail on edge tests\\nEdge cases are input data lying at the boundary of the permissible range or having non-standard properties — an empty list, an array of one element, very large numbers or negative values, for example. Algorithms are often developed and tested on typical data, so the boundary conditions can be missed, which leads to incorrect working.\\nThe main causes of errors\\n\\n- Incorrect initialisation: the variables can receive initial values unsuitable for the edge cases (division by zero, for example).\\n- Errors in the indexing: going beyond the bounds of the array with an empty or single-element set.\\n- Logical miscalculations: the conditions of the comparisons do not take equality or the minimum/maximum values into account.\\n- Overflow: when working with large numbers in languages with a fixed bit width.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nThe corrected variant with a check:\\n\\nHow to avoid problems\\n\\n- Always check the input data for correctness (emptiness, type, range).\\n- Write tests covering the boundary cases: empty structures, minimum/maximum values, negative numbers, zeroes.\\n- Use defensive programming: check the indices before access, handle the exceptions.\\nConclusion: Taking edge tests into account is mandatory for building reliable algorithms. The regular testing of boundary conditions helps reveal hidden errors and raises a program's resilience to non-standard data.","function findMax(arr) {\\n  let max = arr[0]; // An error: if arr is empty, arr[0] = undefined\\n  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    if (arr[i] > max) max = arr[i];\\n  }\\n  return max;\\n}\\n\\n// The edge case: an empty array\\nconsole.log(findMax([])); // undefined, whereas an error or null is expected\\n\\nfunction findMax(arr) {\\n  if (arr.length === 0) return null; // the handling of an empty array\\n  let max = arr[0];\\n  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    if (arr[i] > max) max = arr[i];\\n  }\\n  return max;\\n}"],[930,"Space complexity assesses the quantity of additional memory an algorithm requires in order to run. It is expressed by means of Big O notation — O(1), O(n) or O(n^2), for example. To assess it you have to take into account the memory for the input data, the temporary variables, the recursive calls and the data structures. The analysis helps you choose effective algorithms when resources are limited.","What is space complexity?\\nThe space complexity of an algorithm is a measure of the volume of memory necessary for it to run, depending on the size of the input data. It includes the memory for holding the input data, the temporary variables, the auxiliary data structures and the call stack during recursion. The assessment of space complexity lets you predict how an algorithm will behave with large volumes of data and helps you avoid memory overflow.\\nHow do you assess it?\\nTo assess space complexity, follow these steps:\\n\\n- Determine which variables and data structures are created in the algorithm.\\n- Assess how their size depends on the size of the input data (n).\\n- Ignore the constant multipliers and the lower terms, using Big O notation.\\n- Take the memory for the recursive calls into account (the depth of the recursion).\\nExamples\\nLet us look at several examples in JavaScript:\\n\\nIn the first example only one variable, total, is used, so the memory does not depend on n — O(1). In the second a new array of the size n is created — O(n). In the third a two-dimensional n×n array is created — O(n²).\\nRecursion\\nWith recursion it matters to take the depth of the call stack into account. A recursive factorial, for example:\\n\\nHere the depth of the recursion is equal to n, so the space complexity is O(n) because of the call stack.\\nConclusion\\nThe assessment of space complexity helps you choose algorithms that save memory, especially in environments with limited resources, such as mobile devices or embedded systems. Always take both the time and the space complexity into account for an optimal solution.","// O(1) — constant memory\\nfunction sum(arr) {\\n  let total = 0;\\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    total += arr[i];\\n  }\\n  return total;\\n}\\n\\n// O(n) — linear memory\\nfunction duplicate(arr) {\\n  let newArr = [];\\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    newArr.push(arr[i] * 2);\\n  }\\n  return newArr;\\n}\\n\\n// O(n^2) — quadratic memory\\nfunction createMatrix(n) {\\n  let matrix = [];\\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\n    matrix[i] = [];\\n    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {\\n      matrix[i][j] = i * j;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return matrix;\\n}\\n\\nfunction factorial(n) {\\n  if (n === 0) return 1;\\n  return n * factorial(n - 1);\\n}"],[931,"Boundary cases are non-standard or extreme input data that can cause an algorithm to fail. Handling them includes checking for empty arrays, zero values, negative numbers, duplicates and the maximum possible values. For that, conditional statements, the validation of the input data and the writing of tests covering those scenarios are applied. That makes the code more reliable and predictable.","What are boundary cases and why do they matter?\\nBoundary cases (edge cases) are situations where an algorithm's input data lies at the boundary of the permissible values or has a non-standard format. An empty list, a string of zero length, a number equal to zero, or an array with one element, for example. Ignoring such cases can lead to the unpredictable behaviour of a program, to runtime errors or to incorrect results. The handling of boundary cases is a key part of writing resilient code.\\nThe main approaches to handling them\\n\\n- The validation of the input data: Check the types, the ranges and the formats of the data before carrying out the main logic.\\n- The use of defensive programming: Add checks for null, undefined, empty collections and other extreme values.\\n- The writing of tests: Cover with tests not only the standard scenarios but every possible boundary case.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\nLet us look at a function that searches for the maximum element in an array. Without the handling of the boundary cases it can give an incorrect result or an error.\\n\\nIn this example we check that the array exists and is not empty. If the array is empty, we return null, which lets the calling code handle that situation correctly.\\nConclusion\\nThe handling of boundary cases is a mandatory practice when developing algorithms. It raises the reliability of code, prevents unexpected failures and makes debugging easier. Always take the extreme values of the input data into account and write tests covering those scenarios.","function findMax(arr) {\\n  // The boundary case: an empty array\\n  if (!arr || arr.length === 0) {\\n    return null; // or throw an exception\\n  }\\n  let max = arr[0];\\n  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    if (arr[i] > max) {\\n      max = arr[i];\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return max;\\n}\\n\\n// Examples of calls\\nconsole.log(findMax([3, 7, 2])); // 7\\nconsole.log(findMax([])); // null\\nconsole.log(findMax([5])); // 5"],[932,"The space complexity of the recursive traversal of a tree is O(h), where h is the height of the tree. That is connected with the fact that recursion uses the call stack, whose depth is equal to the height of the tree. In the worst case (a degenerate tree) the complexity can be O(n), where n is the number of nodes.","The space complexity of the recursive traversal of a tree\\nDuring the recursive traversal of a tree (in-order, pre-order, post-order, for example) every recursive call adds a new frame to the call stack. The depth of the stack corresponds to the current depth of the recursion, which is equal to the height of the tree. The space complexity is thus determined by the height of the tree, h.\\nAn example\\nLet us look at a binary search tree. During a recursive in-order traversal:\\n\\nIn a balanced tree the height h = log(n), so the complexity is O(log n). In a degenerate tree (like a linked list) h = n, and the complexity is O(n).\\nApplication\\nThis approach is used in algorithms where the traversal of a tree is required without additional memory beyond the call stack. For large trees with a great height, however, recursion can lead to a stack overflow.\\nConclusion\\nThe space complexity of the recursive traversal of a tree is equal to O(h). It matters to take that into account when working with deep or unbalanced trees, where an iterative traversal with an explicit stack may be required in order to control the memory.","function inorder(node) {\\n  if (node === null) return;\\n  inorder(node.left);\\n  console.log(node.value);\\n  inorder(node.right);\\n}"],[933,"A \`Proxy\` is a special object in JavaScript that lets you intercept the operations carried out on another object (access to properties, their change or deletion, for example). A proxy can be useful for validation, logging or the creation of objects with \\"magic behaviour\\", where the object's behaviour changes depending on the context.","A \`Proxy\` is a built-in object in JavaScript that lets you control access to and the modification of another object or function. That is useful when you have to add logic around the standard operations, such as the reading and writing of an object's properties, the calling of functions and so on. A proxy intercepts those operations by means of \\"traps\\", which can be configured.\\nAn example of creating a Proxy for an object:\\n\\nIn this example we create a proxy for the \`target\` object. If a property does not exist, the proxy returns the string \\"The property has not been found\\" instead of \`undefined\`.\\nA \`Proxy\` is useful for various tasks, such as the validation of input, logging, the automatic creation of values for new properties or the management of access to data. It is also widely used in frameworks such as Vue.js to create reactive data.","const target = {  message: \\"Hello, world!\\" }; \\nconst handler = {\\n  get: function(target, property) {\\n    return property in target ? target[property] : \\"The property has not been found\\";  \\n  } \\n}; \\nconst proxy = new Proxy(target, handler); \\nconsole.log(proxy.message); // \\"Hello, world!\\" \\nconsole.log(proxy.nonExistent); // \\"The property has not been found\\""],[934,"A node is considered to have no descendants if both of its pointers to the left and the right descendant are equal to null. Such nodes are called leaves. This basic property is used during the traversal of a tree and the counting of the leaves.","Determining a node without descendants\\nIn a binary tree every node can have up to two descendants: a left one and a right one. A node is considered to have no descendants (to be a leaf) if both of its pointers to descendants are equal to null. It is a fundamental property, used when analysing the structure of a tree — when counting the number of leaves or when checking whether a tree is complete, for example.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nApplication\\nThe determination of leaf nodes is necessary in algorithms for traversing a tree (DFS, BFS), when computing the height of a tree, and also in tasks connected with the deletion of nodes or with balancing. In binary search trees, for example, the leaves are the nodes that can be safely deleted without breaking the structure.\\nConclusion\\nThe check for the absence of descendants is a basic operation when working with trees. It lets you identify the leaves, which matters for many tree-handling algorithms, from simple counting to complex operations of deletion and balancing.","class TreeNode {\\n  constructor(value) {\\n    this.value = value;\\n    this.left = null;\\n    this.right = null;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction isLeaf(node) {\\n  return node.left === null && node.right === null;\\n}\\n\\n// An example of use\\nconst root = new TreeNode(1);\\nroot.left = new TreeNode(2);\\nroot.right = new TreeNode(3);\\nconsole.log(isLeaf(root)); // false\\nconsole.log(isLeaf(root.left)); // true"],[935,"The time complexity of the traversal of a tree is O(n), where n is the number of nodes. That holds for every main method of traversal: depth-first (DFS) and breadth-first (BFS). Every node is visited exactly once, so the complexity is linear.","The time complexity of the traversal of a tree\\nThe traversal of a tree is the process of visiting every node exactly once. Regardless of the method chosen (pre-order, in-order, post-order for DFS, or level-order for BFS), every node is handled once. That means the time complexity is always linear relative to the number of nodes.\\nWhy O(n)?\\nThe traversal algorithm carries out a constant number of operations per node: the check, the addition to the stack/queue, the printing of the value. The overall time is thus proportional to the number of nodes n. For a binary tree with 1000 nodes, for example, about 1000 steps will be required.\\nAn example of code (DFS in JavaScript)\\n\\nAn example of code (BFS in JavaScript)\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe traversal of a tree always has the linear time complexity O(n), which makes it effective for handling every node. This approach is applied in searching, serialisation, the computation of a tree's height and other tasks where a full traversal of the structure is required.","function dfs(node) {\\n  if (!node) return;\\n  console.log(node.value); // the visiting of the node\\n  dfs(node.left);\\n  dfs(node.right);\\n}\\n// The time complexity: O(n), where n is the number of nodes\\n\\nfunction bfs(root) {\\n  const queue = [root];\\n  while (queue.length) {\\n    const node = queue.shift();\\n    console.log(node.value);\\n    if (node.left) queue.push(node.left);\\n    if (node.right) queue.push(node.right);\\n  }\\n}\\n// The time complexity: O(n)"],[936,"Recursion is the calling of a function from inside itself. The depth of the recursion is the number of nested calls until the base case is reached. Every call adds a frame to the call stack. If the depth is too great, the stack overflows (a stack overflow).","How the depth of recursion works\\nRecursion is the technique by which a function calls itself in order to solve a subtask. The depth of the recursion is the number of successive calls of the function until the base case (the exit condition) is reached. Every call creates a new frame in the call stack, which holds the local variables and the return address.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nIn this example the depth of the recursion is equal to 5 (the calls for n=5,4,3,2,1,0). Every call waits for the result of the next, until it reaches the base case.\\nThe risk of a stack overflow\\nIf the depth of the recursion is too great (factorial(100000), for example), the stack can overflow, since the memory is limited. That is called a stack overflow. In different languages and environments the limits differ (usually several thousand calls).\\nApplication\\n\\n- The traversal of trees and graphs\\n- Sorting algorithms (quicksort, merge sort)\\n- Tasks that naturally break up into subtasks (the factorial, Fibonacci numbers)\\nConclusion\\nRecursion is convenient for tasks with a nested structure, but it requires care as regards the depth. For great depths it is better to use iterative approaches or tail recursion (if the language supports the optimisation).","function factorial(n) {\\n  if (n === 0) return 1; // the base case\\n  return n * factorial(n - 1); // the recursive call\\n}\\nconsole.log(factorial(5)); // 120"],[937,"A leaf of a tree is a node that has no child elements. It is located at the bottom level of the hierarchy. Leaves have no descendants, unlike the internal nodes. They represent the final elements of the data.","What is a leaf of a tree?\\nIn the 'tree' data structure, a leaf node is a node that has no child nodes. It is the end point of a branch, from which no new nodes go further. Leaves are located at the bottom level of the hierarchy and contain no references to other nodes.\\nAn example in a binary tree\\nLet us look at a binary tree, where every node can have up to two descendants. The leaves will be the nodes both of whose descendants are equal to null.\\n\\nApplication\\nLeaves matter during the traversal of a tree (in the DFS and BFS algorithms, for example), when computing the height of a tree, and also in the syntax trees of compilers, where the leaves represent operands or constants.\\nConclusion: An understanding of leaves is necessary for working with any hierarchical data, from file systems to data structures in programming.","class TreeNode {\\n  constructor(value) {\\n    this.value = value;\\n    this.left = null;\\n    this.right = null;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// We create the tree:\\n//     1\\n//    / \\\\\\n//   2   3\\n//  /\\n// 4\\n\\nconst root = new TreeNode(1);\\nroot.left = new TreeNode(2);\\nroot.right = new TreeNode(3);\\nroot.left.left = new TreeNode(4);\\n\\n// The leaves: node 3 (no descendants) and node 4 (no descendants)\\nconsole.log(root.right.left === null && root.right.right === null); // true\\nconsole.log(root.left.left.left === null && root.left.left.right === null); // true"],[938,"If a solution does not pass all of the tests, first read the error messages. Check which tests failed and why. Then analyse the code for logical errors, boundary cases or a mismatch with the requirements. Use a debugger or add temporary output to check the intermediate values. Once you have corrected it, run the tests again.","The analysis of errors in tests\\nWhen your solution does not pass all of the tests, it matters to approach the search for the problem systematically. Begin by reading the error messages — they often point to specific lines of code or to the values expected. Determine which tests failed: possibly they are boundary cases you did not take into account.\\nChecking the logic and the boundary conditions\\nCheck whether you are handling the extreme values, empty input data or large volumes correctly. If a function is supposed to return the sum of numbers, for example, make sure it works with negative numbers and with zero. Use a debugger or temporary \`console.log\`s to track the execution.\\n\\nA comparison with the requirements\\nRe-read the statement of the task. Possibly you understood the requirements incorrectly or missed details. Compare your code with the examples from the statement. If the tests are automatic, check that your function returns the data in the format expected.\\nSummary\\nA systematic approach to debugging helps you find and correct errors quickly. Use the error messages, a debugger and a re-reading of the requirements in order to pass all of the tests successfully.","function sum(a, b) {\\n  console.log('a:', a, 'b:', b);\\n  return a + b;\\n}\\n\\n// An example of a test\\nconsole.log(sum(2, 3)); // 5 is expected\\nconsole.log(sum(-1, 1)); // 0 is expected"],[939,"The recursive traversal of a tree is the method in which a function calls itself for every node. First the current node is handled, then all of its child elements are traversed recursively. That lets you visit every node exactly once. Such an approach is simple to implement and readable, but it can lead to a stack overflow with deep trees.","What is the recursive traversal of a tree?\\nThe recursive traversal of a tree is a way of visiting every node of a tree in sequence, in which the function calls itself in order to handle every child node. It is the natural way of working with hierarchical structures, such as a file system, the DOM tree or JSON objects.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe main idea consists in the fact that for every node we first carry out some action (print the value, for example), and then recursively call the same function for every one of its descendants. The base case is when a node has no children (a leaf), and then the recursion stops.\\nAn example of code in JavaScript\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- The traversal of the DOM tree to search for elements\\n- The serialisation and deserialisation of JSON\\n- Algorithms on graphs (DFS)\\n- The parsing of expressions (an AST)\\nConclusion\\nThe recursive traversal of a tree is a simple, clear way of working with hierarchical data. It is worth applying when the tree is not too deep (in order to avoid a stack overflow) and when the readability of the code matters. For very deep trees it is better to use an iterative approach with an explicit stack.","const tree = {\\n  value: 1,\\n  children: [\\n    { value: 2, children: [] },\\n    {\\n      value: 3,\\n      children: [\\n        { value: 4, children: [] },\\n        { value: 5, children: [] }\\n      ]\\n    }\\n  ]\\n};\\n\\nfunction traverse(node) {\\n  console.log(node.value); // The handling of the node\\n  for (let child of node.children) {\\n    traverse(child); // The recursive call\\n  }\\n}\\n\\ntraverse(tree);\\n// Output: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5"],[940,"In a binary tree every node has no more than two descendants: a left one and a right one. The left branch usually contains values less than the root, the right one greater. This property is used in binary search trees for the effective searching of data.","The basics of a binary tree\\nA binary tree is a hierarchical data structure where every node can have no more than two descendants, which are called the left and the right child. The left and right branches determine the order in which the data is held and accessed.\\nThe difference between the left and the right branch\\nIn a binary search tree (BST) the left branch contains the nodes with keys less than the root's, and the right branch those with keys greater than the root's. This property is applied recursively to every subtree, which lets you carry out a search in O(log n) in a balanced tree.\\nAn example of code in JavaScript\\n\\nApplication\\nThe left and right branches are used in sorting algorithms (an in-order traversal gives a sorted array, for example), in databases for indexing and in the implementation of associative arrays. An understanding of how they are arranged is critical for working with trees.\\nConclusion: the left and right branches are the foundation of the organisation of data in binary trees, ensuring effective searching and sorting. Apply this structure when fast access to ordered data is needed.","class TreeNode {\\n  constructor(value) {\\n    this.value = value;\\n    this.left = null;\\n    this.right = null;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The creation of the tree\\nconst root = new TreeNode(10);\\nroot.left = new TreeNode(5);\\nroot.right = new TreeNode(15);\\n\\n// The search for a value\\nfunction search(node, target) {\\n  if (!node) return false;\\n  if (node.value === target) return true;\\n  if (target < node.value) return search(node.left, target);\\n  return search(node.right, target);\\n}"],[941,"A binary tree is a hierarchical data structure where every node has no more than two descendants: a left one and a right one. It is used for the effective searching, sorting and organisation of data. Examples: a binary search tree (BST) and a heap.","A binary tree is a fundamental data structure in computer science, representing a hierarchy of nodes. Every node contains a value and references to a left and a right descendant (which can be null). The main property: every node has no more than two children.\\nThe main concepts\\n\\n- The root — the top node of the tree.\\n- A leaf — a node without descendants.\\n- The height — the maximum number of edges from the root to a leaf.\\n- The depth — the number of edges from the root to a node.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nApplication\\n\\n- A binary search tree (BST) — for fast searching, insertion and deletion (O(log n) on average).\\n- A heap — for the implementation of priority queues.\\n- Syntax trees — in compilers, for the parsing of expressions.\\nBinary trees lie at the basis of many algorithms, such as depth-first (DFS) and breadth-first (BFS) traversal. They are effective for hierarchical data and for tasks requiring fast access by key.\\nConclusion: A binary tree is worth applying when the data has a hierarchical structure or when fast searching/sorting is required — in databases or file-management systems, for example.","class TreeNode {\\n  constructor(value) {\\n    this.value = value;\\n    this.left = null;\\n    this.right = null;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst root = new TreeNode(10);\\nroot.left = new TreeNode(5);\\nroot.right = new TreeNode(15);\\nroot.left.left = new TreeNode(2);\\nroot.left.right = new TreeNode(7);"],[942,"A node of a tree is an element of a hierarchical data structure. Every node contains a value and references to its child nodes. A node without parents is called the root, and one without children a leaf. Trees are used to represent hierarchies — a file system or the DOM, for example.","What is a node of a tree?\\nA node of a tree is the fundamental element of the \\"tree\\" data structure. A tree represents a hierarchical structure, where every node can have zero or more child nodes. The nodes are joined by edges, forming a directed graph without cycles.\\nThe main characteristics of a node\\n\\n- The value (data) — the information held in the node (a number, a string, an object).\\n- The references to the child nodes — pointers to the next nodes in the hierarchy.\\n- The parent node — the node from which an edge goes to the given node (except for the root).\\nThe types of node\\n\\n- The root — the only node without a parent.\\n- A leaf — a node without child elements.\\n- An internal node — it has at least one descendant.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nThe nodes of trees are used in the DOM (an HTML document), in file systems, in search algorithms (binary trees), in compilers (an AST) and in databases (B-trees).\\nConclusion: An understanding of the node of a tree is necessary for working with any hierarchical data. It is a basic concept, which lies at the basis of many algorithms and systems.","class TreeNode {\\n  constructor(value) {\\n    this.value = value;\\n    this.children = [];\\n  }\\n  addChild(child) {\\n    this.children.push(child);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst root = new TreeNode('A');\\nconst child1 = new TreeNode('B');\\nroot.addChild(child1);\\nconsole.log(root.value); // A\\nconsole.log(root.children[0].value); // B"],[943,"In recursion, for an empty node (null) null or an empty value is usually returned, depending on the task. It is the base case, which stops the recursion. During the traversal of a tree, for example, null is returned in order to end the branch. When counting a sum, 0 is returned. When searching for an element, null or false is returned.","What should you return in recursion for an empty node?\\nIn recursive algorithms working with trees or linked lists, an empty node (null) is the base case. It signals that further recursion is not needed and that the algorithm has to return some value that will correctly end the computations.\\nThe general rules\\n\\n- If the function searches for an element or checks a condition, null or false is returned for null.\\n- If the function computes a sum, a count or another aggregated quantity, the neutral element is returned for null (0 for a sum, 1 for a product, an empty array for the gathering of data).\\n- If the function builds a new data structure, null or an empty structure is returned for null.\\nExamples\\nThe search for an element in a binary search tree:\\n\\nThe counting of the sum of every node of a tree:\\n\\nThe gathering of every value into an array (a depth-first traversal):\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe correct choice of the value returned for an empty node is critical for the correct working of recursion. Always determine which value is neutral for your operation (null, 0, an empty array, false), and use it in the base case. That will ensure the correct ending of the recursion and a correct result.","function searchBST(root, val) {\\n  if (root === null) return null; // the base case\\n  if (root.val === val) return root;\\n  if (val < root.val) return searchBST(root.left, val);\\n  return searchBST(root.right, val);\\n}\\n\\nfunction sumTree(root) {\\n  if (root === null) return 0; // the neutral element for a sum\\n  return root.val + sumTree(root.left) + sumTree(root.right);\\n}\\n\\nfunction collectValues(root) {\\n  if (root === null) return []; // an empty array for the concatenation\\n  return [root.val, ...collectValues(root.left), ...collectValues(root.right)];\\n}"],[944,"A polyfill is a piece of code (usually JavaScript) that provides modern functionality in older browsers that do not support it. It imitates the new behaviour using old technologies, which lets you support a broader range of browsers.","A polyfill is a code \\"patch\\" that adds support for new capabilities of the browser, such as modern methods or APIs, in older browsers that do not support them. Polyfills help developers use the newest features of JavaScript or CSS without worrying that their code will not work in obsolete browsers.\\nAn example of a polyfill for the \`Array.prototype.includes\` method:\\n\\nThis code checks whether the browser supports the \`includes\` method. If it does not, it adds a polyfill that uses \`indexOf\` to achieve the same functionality.\\nPolyfills matter for ensuring cross-browser compatibility, which is especially relevant in corporate environments, where old versions of browsers may be used. Instead of giving up modern capabilities, developers can use polyfills so that their code works everywhere.","if (!Array.prototype.includes) {\\n  Array.prototype.includes = function(value) {\\n    return this.indexOf(value) !== -1;  \\n  }; \\n}"],[945,"The analysis of errors in an algorithm begins with reproducing the problem and gathering data. Use a debugger for the step-by-step execution of the code and the checking of the values of the variables. Add logging of the key stages and check the boundary cases. Compare the expected and the actual behaviour, in order to localise the error.","The main methods of analysing errors in an algorithm\\nThe analysis of errors in an algorithm is the systematic process of revealing and correcting discrepancies between the expected and the actual behaviour of a program. It involves several stages, beginning with the reproduction of the error and ending with its correction and verification.\\nReproduction and the gathering of information\\nThe first step is to reproduce the error stably. For that you have to determine the exact input data that raises it. Then you should gather as much information as possible: the error messages, the values of the variables, the state of the call stack. Use logging in order to record the key moments of the algorithm's execution.\\nStep-by-step debugging and analysis\\nBy means of a debugger (in an IDE, for example) go through the algorithm step by step. Watch the changes of the variables and the conditions of the transitions. Compare the current state with the expected one. Give special attention to the boundary cases: empty arrays, zero values, maximum and minimum numbers.\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at a binary-search algorithm that does not always find the element:\\n\\nIn this example the error may be in the incorrect computation of mid or in the updating of the bounds. Using a debugger, you can notice that under certain conditions left and right do not converge. The correction: make sure mid is computed correctly and that the bounds are updated correctly.\\nConclusion\\nThe systematic analysis of errors by means of debugging, logging and the checking of the boundary cases lets you find and correct problems in algorithms quickly. This approach is especially useful when working with complex data structures and recursive functions.","function binarySearch(arr, target) {\\n  let left = 0;\\n  let right = arr.length - 1;\\n  while (left <= right) {\\n    let mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);\\n    if (arr[mid] === target) return mid;\\n    if (arr[mid] < target) left = mid + 1;\\n    else right = mid - 1;\\n  }\\n  return -1;\\n}\\n\\n// The error: an incorrect updating of the bounds\\nconsole.log(binarySearch([1, 3, 5, 7], 5)); // 2 is expected, but it may be -1"],[946,"To compute the sum of the values in subtrees, a recursive depth-first (post-order) traversal is used. First the sums for the left and the right subtree are computed recursively, then the value of the current node is added to them. The result is returned upwards, which lets you obtain the sum for every node.","The concept of computing the sum in subtrees\\nThe task consists in finding, for every node of a binary tree, the sum of all of the values in its subtree, the node itself included. It is a classic example of the application of a recursive depth-first traversal (DFS) in post-order, where the children are handled first and the parent afterwards.\\nHow it works\\nThe algorithm descends recursively to the leaves and computes the sum for every node by adding the values of the left and the right subtree to its own value. The result is saved or returned. Such an approach guarantees that every node is counted exactly once, and the complexity is O(n), where n is the number of nodes.\\nAn example of code in JavaScript\\n\\nApplication\\nThis method is used in tasks of balancing trees, of analysing data (the computation of the average over subtrees, for example), and also in algorithms where the aggregation of information over a hierarchy is required (file systems, the DOM tree).\\nConclusion: The recursive computation of the sums of subtrees is a fundamental technique for working with trees, which is easily adapted to any aggregating function (the maximum, the minimum, the number of nodes). Apply it everywhere you have to obtain summary information over a hierarchical structure.","class TreeNode {\\n  constructor(val) {\\n    this.val = val;\\n    this.left = null;\\n    this.right = null;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction computeSubtreeSums(root) {\\n  if (!root) return 0;\\n  const leftSum = computeSubtreeSums(root.left);\\n  const rightSum = computeSubtreeSums(root.right);\\n  root.subtreeSum = leftSum + rightSum + root.val;\\n  return root.subtreeSum;\\n}\\n\\n// An example of use:\\nconst root = new TreeNode(1);\\nroot.left = new TreeNode(2);\\nroot.right = new TreeNode(3);\\ncomputeSubtreeSums(root);\\nconsole.log(root.subtreeSum); // 6\\nconsole.log(root.left.subtreeSum); // 2\\nconsole.log(root.right.subtreeSum); // 3"],[947,"The recursive traversal of a tree is the method in which a function calls itself for every node. First the current node is handled, then all of its child nodes are traversed recursively. That lets you visit every node exactly once. Such an approach is natural for trees, since their structure is recursive by its very nature.","The main idea of the recursive traversal of a tree\\nThe recursive traversal of a tree is a way of visiting every node of a tree in sequence, in which the function calls itself in order to handle the subtrees. A tree consists of a root node and subtrees, each of which is itself a tree. It is precisely this self-similar structure that makes recursion the ideal tool for the traversal.\\nThe types of traversal\\nThere are several variants of recursive traversal depending on the order in which the nodes are handled:\\n\\n- Pre-order: the current node is handled first, then the child nodes are traversed recursively.\\n- In-order: the left subtree is traversed first, then the current node, then the right subtree (relevant for binary trees).\\n- Post-order: the child nodes are traversed recursively first, then the current node is handled.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\nLet us look at the traversal of a binary search tree with the printing of the values in ascending order (an in-order traversal):\\n\\nFor a tree with the root 10, the left descendant 5 and the right 15, the call \`inOrderTraversal(root)\` will print: 5, 10, 15.\\nWhere it is applied\\nRecursive traversal is used in:\\n\\n- Work with the DOM tree (the search for elements by class, for example).\\n- File systems (the recursive copying or deletion of folders).\\n- Algorithms on graphs (depth-first search).\\n- Compilers (the traversal of an abstract syntax tree).\\nConclusion\\nThe recursive traversal of a tree is a simple, expressive way of handling hierarchical data. It is worth applying when the data structure is naturally recursive and the depth of the tree does not exceed the permissible size of the call stack (usually up to several thousand levels).","function inOrderTraversal(node) {\\n  if (node === null) return;\\n  inOrderTraversal(node.left);   // the traversal of the left subtree\\n  console.log(node.value);       // the handling of the current node\\n  inOrderTraversal(node.right);  // the traversal of the right subtree\\n}"],[948,"The results of recursive calls are combined after the return from every call. Usually that is done by means of the operations of addition, concatenation or the adding to an array. In the task of traversing a tree, for example, we gather the values of the nodes into a list by recursively traversing the left and the right subtree and then combining them.","The combining of the results of recursive calls\\nRecursion is the technique by which a function calls itself in order to solve subtasks. Once every recursive call has finished, its result has to be combined with the results of the other calls, in order to obtain the final answer. This process is called the merging or combining of the results.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Accumulation through a parameter: the passing of the accumulating result as an argument.\\n- Return and combine: every call returns part of the result, which is then combined at the upper level.\\n- The use of a global variable: the recursive function directly modifies an outer data structure.\\nAn example in JavaScript: the traversal of a tree\\n\\nIn this example every call returns an array, which is then combined by means of the spread operator. It is a typical pattern for tasks connected with trees.\\nAn example in Python: the computation of the sum of the elements of a list\\n\\nHere the result of every call is a number, which is added to the current element. It is the simplest case of combining through an arithmetic operation.\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- «Divide and conquer» algorithms (quicksort, merge sort).\\n- The traversal of graphs and trees (the search for a path, the gathering of data).\\n- Combinatorics tasks (the generation of permutations, combinations).\\nConclusion\\nThe combining of the results of recursive calls is a fundamental technique, which lets you solve tasks effectively by breaking them up into subtasks. This approach is especially useful when working with hierarchical data structures and with algorithms where the aggregation of information from different branches is required.","function collectValues(node) {\\n  if (!node) return [];\\n  // We recursively gather the values from the left and the right subtree\\n  const leftValues = collectValues(node.left);\\n  const rightValues = collectValues(node.right);\\n  // We combine: the current value + the left ones + the right ones\\n  return [node.value, ...leftValues, ...rightValues];\\n}\\n\\ndef sum_list(arr):\\n    if not arr:\\n        return 0\\n    # We recursively sum the tail of the list\\n    rest_sum = sum_list(arr[1:])\\n    # We combine: the first element + the sum of the rest\\n    return arr[0] + rest_sum"],[949,"A race condition is the situation where the result of a program's work depends on the order in which the threads or processes are carried out. It arises with the simultaneous access to shared data without synchronisation. That can lead to unpredictable errors that are hard to reproduce. To prevent it, mutexes, semaphores or atomic operations are used.","What is a race condition?\\nA race condition is a defect in a multithreaded or asynchronous program, where the behaviour of the system depends on the unpredictable order in which the threads are carried out. It happens when two or more threads access shared data at the same time and at least one of them carries out a write, without due synchronisation.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\nLet us look at a simple counter that is increased by two asynchronous operations:\\n\\nBoth threads read counter as 0, then both write 1. The expected result of 2 is not achieved.\\nHow do you avoid it?\\n\\n- Use mutexes (locks) for the critical sections.\\n- Apply atomic operations (AtomicInteger in Java, for example).\\n- Use functional approaches (immutable data).\\nConclusion\\nA race condition is one of the most insidious errors in parallel programming. It is hard to catch, since it appears unstably. Always synchronise access to shared resources, especially in highly loaded systems.","let counter = 0;\\n\\nasync function increment() {\\n  let temp = counter;\\n  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 100));\\n  counter = temp + 1;\\n}\\n\\nawait Promise.all([increment(), increment()]);\\nconsole.log(counter); // It may be 1 rather than 2"],[950,"Pure SQL queries can be more performant than an ORM, because an ORM adds the overheads of converting objects, generating queries and managing the cache. An ORM often creates non-optimal queries — the N+1 problem, for example, where a separate query is carried out for every related entity. Direct SQL lets you control precisely which data is extracted and how, minimising the number of queries and the volume of data transferred.","Why pure SQL queries can be more performant than an ORM\\nAn ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) is a convenient tool for working with databases through objects, but it hides the details of how the queries are carried out. That can lead to a reduction in performance because of the additional overheads. Pure SQL queries give the developer full control over what is carried out and how, which often lets you achieve a better speed.\\nThe main causes of the difference in performance\\n\\n- The overheads of conversion: an ORM spends time mapping the results of a query into objects and back. That is especially noticeable when working with large volumes of data.\\n- The generation of non-optimal queries: an ORM can create superfluous or slow queries — the N+1 problem, for example, where for every record from the first table a separate query to the related table is carried out.\\n- The absence of control over the execution plan: an ORM does not always use the indexes optimally, or it may not take the specifics of the database into account (window functions, CTEs, for example).\\nAn example of a comparison\\nSuppose you have to obtain every user and their latest orders. With an ORM (Django ORM, for example) that can look like this:\\n\\nThat will lead to an N+1 query. Pure SQL solves it in a single query:\\n\\nSuch a query is carried out faster, since the database can optimise its execution by using the indexes and the joins.\\nWhen is it worth using pure SQL?\\n\\n- For complex reports and analytics, where the aggregation of data is required.\\n- When working with large volumes of data, where every millisecond matters.\\n- For operations of mass updating or insertion, where an ORM can be too slow.\\nConclusion\\nPure SQL queries are preferable in scenarios where performance is critical and the complexity of the data is high. An ORM is convenient for simple CRUD operations and fast development, but for optimisation it is worth using direct SQL or combining both approaches.","# ORM: the N+1 problem\\nusers = User.objects.all()\\nfor user in users:\\n    last_order = user.orders.order_by('-created_at').first()  # a separate query for every user\\n\\n-- SQL: a single query with a subquery\\nSELECT u.*, o.*\\nFROM users u\\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON u.id = o.user_id\\nWHERE o.created_at = (\\n    SELECT MAX(created_at) FROM orders WHERE user_id = u.id\\n);"],[951,"To limit parallel async tasks, a pool of promises or libraries of the p-limit kind are used. A queue of tasks is created and a limited number is started at the same time. When one task finishes, the next one from the queue is started. That prevents the system from being overloaded and controls the use of resources.","The limiting of parallel async tasks\\nWhen you have to carry out many asynchronous operations (requests to an API, for example), starting them all at the same time can overload the network or the server. To control it, a mechanism of limiting the concurrency is used.\\nHow it works\\nA queue of tasks and a pool of N simultaneously executing promises are created. When one promise finishes, the next is taken from the queue. That is similar to the working of a thread pool in other languages.\\nAn example of the implementation\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- The loading of a multitude of files\\n- Batch requests to an API with rate limiting\\n- The processing of data in streams\\nConclusion: The limiting of concurrency is an important pattern for the stability and the performance of applications, especially when working with external resources or limited systems.","async function asyncPool(limit, tasks) {\\n  const results = [];\\n  const executing = new Set();\\n\\n  for (const task of tasks) {\\n    const p = Promise.resolve().then(() => task());\\n    results.push(p);\\n    executing.add(p);\\n\\n    const clean = () => executing.delete(p);\\n    p.then(clean, clean);\\n\\n    if (executing.size >= limit) {\\n      await Promise.race(executing);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  return Promise.all(results);\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\nconst tasks = [1,2,3,4,5].map(i => () => fetch(\`/api/\${i}\`));\\nawait asyncPool(2, tasks);"],[952,"A timeout in async requests is needed in order to limit the time spent waiting for a response from the server. If the server does not respond within the period given, the request is automatically interrupted. That prevents the application from hanging and lets you handle the error. Without a timeout a request can wait for a response endlessly, blocking the user interface.","What is a timeout in async requests for?\\nA timeout is a mechanism that limits the maximum time spent waiting for a response from the server while an asynchronous HTTP request is carried out. If the server does not respond within the interval given, the request is automatically interrupted and the handling of the error is carried out. That is critically important for building responsive, stable applications, especially in the conditions of an unstable network or of overloaded servers.\\nHow does it work?\\nIn JavaScript, to implement a timeout you can use the built-in \`AbortController\` object in combination with \`fetch\`. \`AbortController\` lets you cancel a request at any moment. The timer is set by means of \`setTimeout\`, which calls the controller's \`abort()\` method once the time given has expired.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- In web applications, to prevent the UI from hanging with slow requests.\\n- In microservice architecture, to limit the time spent waiting for a response from other services.\\n- In mobile applications, to save traffic and battery.\\nConclusion\\nA timeout is a mandatory element of any asynchronous interaction with external resources. It raises an application's fault tolerance, improves the user experience and prevents leaks of resources. Use a timeout always when making requests to external APIs or databases.","async function fetchWithTimeout(url, timeoutMs = 5000) {\\n  const controller = new AbortController();\\n  const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), timeoutMs);\\n\\n  try {\\n    const response = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });\\n    clearTimeout(timeoutId); // we clear the timer on success\\n    return await response.json();\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    if (error.name === 'AbortError') {\\n      throw new Error('The request exceeded the waiting time');\\n    }\\n    throw error;\\n  }\\n}"],[953,"Pydantic's model_validate can cause lazy loading if the object passed is an instance of an ORM (SQLAlchemy, for example) with relations that have not been loaded. During the validation Pydantic tries to gain access to every field of the model, the relations included, which forces the ORM to carry out additional queries to the database in order to load that data.","Why can model_validate cause lazy loading?\\nPydantic is a library for the validation of data, which is often used together with ORMs such as SQLAlchemy. When you pass an ORM object into \`model_validate\`, Pydantic tries to validate every field stated in the schema. If the schema has fields corresponding to related entities (\`author: AuthorSchema\`, for example) and the ORM object has not yet loaded those relations (lazy loading), then an additional query to the database will happen when they are accessed.\\nAn example\\n\\nHow do you avoid it?\\n\\n- Use \`selectinload\` or \`joinedload\` for the explicit loading of the relations.\\n- Configure Pydantic to work with the ORM through \`from_attributes=True\`.\\n- Use \`model_validate\` only with data that has already been loaded.\\nConclusion: \`model_validate\` can cause lazy loading if the schema includes the fields of related entities and the ORM object has not loaded them in advance. It matters to take that into account when designing an API, in order to avoid unexpected queries to the database and a reduction in performance.","from pydantic import BaseModel\\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import Session\\n\\nclass AuthorSchema(BaseModel):\\n    id: int\\n    name: str\\n\\nclass BookSchema(BaseModel):\\n    id: int\\n    title: str\\n    author: AuthorSchema\\n\\n# The ORM model\\nclass Book(Base):\\n    __tablename__ = 'books'\\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)\\n    title = Column(String)\\n    author_id = Column(Integer, ForeignKey('authors.id'))\\n    author = relationship('Author', lazy='select')  # lazy loading\\n\\n# Use\\nbook = session.get(Book, 1)  # author has not yet been loaded\\nbook_schema = BookSchema.model_validate(book)  # here a query to authors will happen"],[954,"Lazy loading loads the related data only when it is first accessed, which can lead to the problem of N+1 queries. Eager loading loads all of the related data at once by means of a JOIN or separate queries, which reduces the number of queries to the database. The choice between them depends on the scenario of use: lazy suits rare access to the relations, eager suits cases where the data will certainly be needed.","The main concepts\\nLazy loading and eager loading are two strategies for loading related data in object-relational mapping (ORM). They determine when and how the related entities are loaded from the database.\\nLazy loading\\nWith lazy loading the related data is loaded only at the moment it is first accessed. If you have a User object with a collection of Orders, for example, then when the user is obtained the orders are not loaded. They will be loaded by a separate query when you call user.orders. That saves resources if the related data is not needed, but it can lead to the problem of N+1 queries, where for every parent object an additional query is carried out.\\n\\nEager loading\\nWith eager loading the related data is loaded at once together with the parent object, usually through a JOIN or separate queries. That increases the volume of data in a single query but reduces the overall number of queries. It suits cases where you know for certain that the related data will be needed.\\n\\nWhen to use what\\n\\n- Lazy loading: when the related data is needed rarely or not for every object. The loading of the comments on an article only when they are viewed, for example.\\n- Eager loading: when the related data is needed for most objects. The loading of the author together with the post for the display on a list page, for example.\\nConclusion: The choice between lazy and eager loading depends on the patterns of access to the data. For APIs with predictable requests, eager loading is more often used, in order to avoid the N+1 problem. For complex graphs of objects with rare access to the relations, lazy loading can be more effective.","// An example in TypeORM (Node.js)\\nconst user = await userRepository.findOne({ where: { id: 1 } });\\n// The orders have not been loaded\\nconsole.log(user.orders); // Here an additional query is carried out\\n\\n// An example in TypeORM\\nconst user = await userRepository.findOne({\\n  where: { id: 1 },\\n  relations: ['orders']\\n});\\n// The orders have already been loaded\\nconsole.log(user.orders); // No additional query"],[955,"\`requestAnimationFrame\` is a method in JavaScript that tells the browser that an animation has to be carried out and asks the browser to call the function given in order to update the animation before the repaint. It synchronises with the screen's refresh rate, which makes the animation smooth and optimised. Instead of using \`setTimeout\` or \`setInterval\` for an animation, \`requestAnimationFrame\` provides better management of performance and energy consumption.","\`requestAnimationFrame\` is a method for creating smooth animations in the browser. Unlike \`setTimeout\` or \`setInterval\`, it automatically synchronises with the screen's refresh rate (usually 60 frames per second), which makes the animation smoother. This method also helps the browser suspend the animation if the tab is not active, which reduces the load on the system.\\nAn example of the use of \`requestAnimationFrame\` to create a simple animation of a moving block:\\n\\nThis example updates the element's position smoothly as the time progresses, using \`requestAnimationFrame\`. The use of this method helps save the browser's resources and maintain high performance. It is widely applied when creating interactive animations, games and dynamic user interfaces.","let start = null; \\nconst element = document.getElementById(\\"animatedBlock\\"); \\nfunction animate(timestamp) {\\n  if (!start) start = timestamp;  \\n  let progress = timestamp - start;  \\n  element.style.transform = \`translateX(\${Math.min(progress / 10, 200)}px)\`;    \\n  if (progress < 2000) {\\n     // The animation lasts 2 seconds    \\n     requestAnimationFrame(animate);  \\n  } \\n} \\nrequestAnimationFrame(animate);"],[956,"When 10,000 async tasks are started at the same time, they are not carried out in parallel but are put into the microtask queue. The event loop handles them in sequence, which can lead to the interface hanging and to stack-overflow errors. To manage a large number of tasks, libraries of the p-limit kind or queues with concurrency control are used.","How the execution of async tasks works\\nWhen you start 10,000 asynchronous tasks at the same time, they are not carried out in parallel in different threads. JavaScript uses a single-threaded model with an event loop. Every async function returns a Promise, and when they are called they are put into the microtask queue. The event loop sequentially extracts the tasks from that queue and carries them out one after another.\\nThe problems with a large number of tasks\\n\\n- The blocking of the event loop: while all of the microtasks are being handled, the main thread cannot react to the user's events, which leads to the interface hanging.\\n- The overflow of the call stack: if the tasks synchronously create new microtasks, a Maximum call stack size exceeded error can arise.\\n- Memory leaks: a large number of unfulfilled Promises can take up a lot of memory.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nSuch an approach is used when handling large volumes of data, making mass requests to an API, loading files or parsing. Libraries of the p-limit or async kind, or your own implementations of queues, help you avoid the problems.\\nConclusion: Starting 10,000 async tasks without concurrency control can block the event loop and lead to the application hanging. Use the limiting of concurrency for the effective management of resources and the maintenance of the interface's responsiveness.","// The problematic approach\\nconst tasks = Array(10000).fill().map(() => asyncTask());\\nawait Promise.all(tasks); // Blocks the event loop\\n\\n// The solution with concurrency control\\nasync function runWithLimit(tasks, limit) {\\n  const results = [];\\n  const executing = new Set();\\n  for (const task of tasks) {\\n    const p = task().then(result => {\\n      executing.delete(p);\\n      return result;\\n    });\\n    executing.add(p);\\n    results.push(p);\\n    if (executing.size >= limit) {\\n      await Promise.race(executing);\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return Promise.all(results);\\n}\\n\\nawait runWithLimit(tasks, 10); // Only 10 tasks are carried out at the same time"],[957,"An ORM hides the generation of SQL, which can lead to unexpected queries, such as the N+1 problem, where a separate query is carried out for every related entity. Lazy loading aggravates that by carrying out queries only when a property is accessed. A developer may not notice those queries until the load grows, which leads to a collapse of performance.","How an ORM can hide problems of a database's performance\\nAn ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) makes work with a database simpler by letting a developer operate on objects instead of SQL queries. This abstraction can hide ineffective queries, however, which become noticeable only under load. The main problem is that the ORM generates the SQL automatically, and the developer may not see the real number of queries.\\nThe N+1 query problem\\nThe classic example is the lazy loading of related entities. When obtaining a list of users and their orders, for example, the ORM may carry out one query for the users and then a separate query for the orders of every user. That leads to N+1 queries, where N is the number of users.\\n\\nImplicit JOINs and superfluous data\\nAn ORM can carry out JOINs without them being explicitly stated, loading superfluous data. With eager loading, for example, the ORM may load every related entity even if they are not needed, which increases the volume of data transferred and the execution time.\\n\\nThe absence of control over the indexes\\nAn ORM generates queries that may not use the indexes, if the developer does not optimise them. An ORM can create queries with LIKE '%text%', for example, which cannot use B-tree indexes, which leads to a full scan of the table.\\nConclusion\\nAn ORM is a powerful tool for speeding up development, but its use requires an understanding of SQL and the monitoring of the queries. Apply an ORM for simple CRUD operations, but for complex reports or highly loaded systems use raw SQL queries or profile the ORM's queries by means of tools of the EXPLAIN kind.","// An example in TypeScript with TypeORM\\nconst users = await userRepository.find(); // 1 query\\nfor (const user of users) {\\n  console.log(user.orders); // N queries (lazy loading)\\n}\\n\\n// An example of eager loading\\nconst users = await userRepository.find({ relations: ['orders', 'profile'] });\\n// Loads all of the related data, even if only the names are needed"],[958,"Idempotency is the property of an operation whereby the repeated carrying out of one and the same request leads to the same result as carrying it out once. A PUT request establishing the value of a resource is idempotent, for example, whereas a POST creating a new resource is not. That matters for the reliability of systems, especially with the repeated sending of requests because of network errors.","What is idempotency?\\nIdempotency is the property of an operation whereby carrying it out once and carrying it out many times with the same input data leads to one and the same state of the system. Simply put, if you carry out a request once or ten times, the result will be the same. That does not mean the server's response will always be identical (the response code may differ, for example), but the final state of the resource will not change after the first successful execution.\\nWhere is it applied?\\nThe concept of idempotency is critically important when designing APIs, especially in distributed systems and microservice architecture. It lets you safely repeat requests during network failures, timeouts or errors on the client's side without fearing undesirable side effects (the double debiting of money, for example).\\nExamples of idempotent and non-idempotent HTTP methods\\n\\n- GET — idempotent. Obtaining data does not change the state of the resource.\\n- PUT — idempotent. The establishment of a specific value of a resource. A repeated request with the same data will not change the state.\\n- DELETE — idempotent. The deletion of a resource. The repeated deletion of the same resource (if it has already been deleted) usually returns an error, but the state of the system (the resource is absent) does not change.\\n- POST — not idempotent. The creation of a new resource. Every request creates a new resource.\\n- PATCH — can be either idempotent or not, depending on the implementation. The operation \\"increase the counter by 1\\", for example, is not idempotent.\\nA practical example in JavaScript (an imitation of an API)\\n\\nConclusion\\nIdempotency is a fundamental property for building reliable, fault-tolerant systems. It should be applied when designing APIs, especially for operations that change state, in order to guarantee the consistency of the data and make the handling of errors on the client's side simpler.","// An idempotent PUT request\\nasync function updateUser(id, data) {\\n  const response = await fetch(\`/api/users/\${id}\`, {\\n    method: 'PUT',\\n    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n    body: JSON.stringify(data)\\n  });\\n  return response.json();\\n}\\n\\n// Let us call it 3 times with the same data\\nawait updateUser(1, { name: 'Alice' });\\nawait updateUser(1, { name: 'Alice' });\\nawait updateUser(1, { name: 'Alice' });\\n// The result: the name of the user with id=1 will always be 'Alice'"],[959,"Once the session has been closed, the object moves into the detached state. If the relationship is configured with lazy='select' (the default), then when it is accessed SQLAlchemy tries to carry out a new query to the database, but the session has already been closed. A DetachedInstanceError exception (sqlalchemy.orm.exc.DetachedInstanceError) arises.","The problem of accessing a relationship after the session has been closed\\nIn SQLAlchemy the session manages the state of the objects and their link with the database. When the session is closed, every object that was loaded through it moves into the detached state. That means they are no longer tied to any session and cannot automatically load additional data from the database.\\nHow the error arises\\nBy default a relationship uses the lazy='select' strategy. That means the related data is loaded only when the attribute is first accessed. If you access a relationship after the session has been closed, SQLAlchemy tries to carry out a new SQL query but finds no active session, which leads to a DetachedInstanceError exception.\\n\\nHow to avoid the error\\n\\n- Explicit loading (eager loading): use \`joinedload\` or \`subqueryload\` to load the related data together with the main query.\\n- Work within the session: access the relationship before the session is closed.\\n- Reattachment: you can add the object back into a new session through \`session.add(user)\`, but that is not always convenient.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse the explicit loading of related data if you plan to work with the object after the session has been closed. That is especially useful in web applications, where objects can be passed to the client or used outside the context of the request.","from sqlalchemy import create_engine, Column, Integer, String, ForeignKey\\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import declarative_base, relationship, sessionmaker\\n\\nBase = declarative_base()\\n\\nclass User(Base):\\n    __tablename__ = 'users'\\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)\\n    name = Column(String)\\n    posts = relationship('Post')\\n\\nclass Post(Base):\\n    __tablename__ = 'posts'\\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)\\n    title = Column(String)\\n    user_id = Column(Integer, ForeignKey('users.id'))\\n\\nengine = create_engine('sqlite:///:memory:')\\nBase.metadata.create_all(engine)\\nSession = sessionmaker(bind=engine)\\nsession = Session()\\n\\nuser = session.query(User).first()\\nsession.close()\\n\\n# The error: DetachedInstanceError\\nprint(user.posts)\\n\\n# An example with eager loading\\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import joinedload\\n\\nsession = Session()\\nuser = session.query(User).options(joinedload(User.posts)).first()\\nsession.close()\\n\\n# Now posts have already been loaded, and there will be no error\\nprint(user.posts)"],[960,"asyncio.gather starts every coroutine passed at the same time without a limit on the number. If you pass thousands of coroutines, each of which carries out a network request, that can exhaust the limits of open connections or the memory, or overload an external service. To control the load, semaphores or libraries of the asyncio-throttle kind are used.","Why can asyncio.gather create too great a load?\\nasyncio.gather is a convenient tool for the parallel execution of several coroutines. It provides no built-in mechanisms for limiting the concurrency, however. If you pass a list of hundreds or thousands of coroutines into gather, they will all be started practically at the same time. That can lead to several problems.\\nThe main risks\\n\\n- The exhaustion of system resources: Every coroutine can open a network connection or a file, or allocate memory. The simultaneous starting of thousands of such operations can exceed the limits of the operating system (the maximum number of open file descriptors, for example).\\n- The overloading of external services: If the coroutines carry out HTTP requests to one API, the server may not cope with such a number of simultaneous requests and may start rejecting them or responding with errors.\\n- The blocking of the event loop: Although asyncio uses cooperative multitasking, too large a number of active coroutines can slow the event loop down, especially if they carry out long input-output operations.\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nIn this example 10,000 coroutines will try to open connections to example.com at the same time. That can lead to connection errors or to the client crashing.\\nHow to avoid the overload\\nTo control the concurrency, use asyncio.Semaphore or wrapper libraries:\\n\\nYou can also use asyncio.wait with the parameter return_when=FIRST_COMPLETED and handle the results as they finish, but gather with a semaphore is the simplest, most reliable way.\\nConclusion\\nasyncio.gather suits small groups of coroutines, but with a mass start always use the limiting of concurrency. That will protect both your system and the external services from overload.","import asyncio\\nimport aiohttp\\n\\nasync def fetch(session, url):\\n    async with session.get(url) as response:\\n        return await response.text()\\n\\nasync def main():\\n    urls = [\\"http://example.com\\"] * 10000  # 10,000 identical URLs\\n    async with aiohttp.ClientSession() as session:\\n        tasks = [fetch(session, url) for url in urls]\\n        results = await asyncio.gather(*tasks)  # Starts them all at once\\n\\nimport asyncio\\n\\nsemaphore = asyncio.Semaphore(100)  # We limit it to 100 simultaneous tasks\\n\\nasync def limited_fetch(session, url):\\n    async with semaphore:\\n        async with session.get(url) as response:\\n            return await response.text()\\n\\nasync def main():\\n    urls = [\\"http://example.com\\"] * 10000\\n    async with aiohttp.ClientSession() as session:\\n        tasks = [limited_fetch(session, url) for url in urls]\\n        results = await asyncio.gather(*tasks)"],[961,"When searching for bugs the frontend and the backend interact through HTTP requests. The developer checks which data is sent and arrives, using the developer tools in the browser. If the error is on the server's side, the logs and the status codes of the responses are looked at. That helps determine where exactly the problem happened.","The interaction of the frontend and the backend when searching for bugs\\nWhen a bug arises, it matters to understand on which side it happened. The frontend sends requests to the backend through an API, usually using HTTP. If the response is unexpected, you have to check both the request and the response.\\nThe tools of diagnosis\\n\\n- The developer tools in the browser (the Network tab) show every request, its headers, body and status code.\\n- The logs on the server help you see how the request is handled and which errors arise.\\n- Postman or curl let you test the API independently of the frontend.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of the interaction of the frontend and the backend helps you localise a bug quickly and correct it on the right side, saving the team's time and resources.","// Frontend: the sending of the request\\nfetch('/api/data')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      throw new Error('Server error: ' + response.status);\\n    }\\n    return response.json();\\n  })\\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Fetch error:', error));\\n\\n// Backend (Node.js/Express): the handling of the request\\napp.get('/api/data', (req, res) => {\\n  try {\\n    const data = getData();\\n    res.json(data);\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    console.error('Backend error:', err);\\n    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });\\n  }\\n});"],[962,"A task passes through the stages: the analysis of the requirements, design, development, code review, testing, deployment and monitoring. At every stage the task is clarified and checked. That helps you avoid errors and improve the quality of the product.","The life cycle of a task in development\\nEvery task in the development process passes through several successive stages, which ensure quality control and consistency with the requirements. An understanding of these stages helps a developer plan the work effectively and interact with the team.\\nThe main stages\\n\\n- The analysis of the requirements — the clarification of the task with the customer or the analyst, the determination of the acceptance criteria.\\n- Design — the creation of the architectural solution, the choice of technologies and approaches.\\n- Development — the writing of the code, the implementation of the functionality.\\n- Code review — the checking of the code by colleagues in order to reveal errors and improve the style.\\n- Testing — manual or automated testing, the checking of conformity to the requirements.\\n- Deployment — the release to staging or production.\\n- Monitoring — the tracking of the feature's working in production, the gathering of feedback.\\nAn example of the process in Git\\n\\nConclusion: following the stages of a task's life cycle reduces the risks, improves the quality of the code and makes teamwork simpler. This approach is useful in any project, especially when using Agile or Scrum.","git checkout -b feature/new-login\\n# the development\\ngit add .\\ngit commit -m \\"Add new login form\\"\\ngit push origin feature/new-login\\n# the creation of the Pull Request\\n# the code review and the tests\\n# the merge into main\\ngit checkout main\\ngit merge feature/new-login\\ngit push origin main\\n# the deployment through CI/CD"],[963,"Screenshot testing is a method of automated testing in which a screenshot of the interface is taken and compared with a reference image. If the pixels differ, the test is considered to have failed. That helps you discover undesirable visual changes quickly after changes in the code. The tests are run in CI/CD to control regressions.","What is screenshot testing?\\nScreenshot testing (or visual regression testing) is an approach to testing a user interface in which screenshots of screens or components are created automatically and compared with reference images saved in advance. If differences in the pixels are discovered, the test is considered to have failed, which signals a possible visual bug.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe process usually includes three steps:\\n\\n- The creation of the reference screenshot (the baseline) — the first successful run of the test.\\n- The running of the test after changes — a new screenshot is taken.\\n- The comparison of the pixels — if the difference exceeds the threshold, the test fails.\\nAn example in JavaScript with the Playwright library:\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nThis method is especially useful in projects with frequent changes of the UI — in design systems or large web applications, for example. It helps you reveal regressions quickly that unit tests do not catch (the shifting of a button or a change of colour, for example).\\nConclusion\\nScreenshot testing is worth applying when the visual consistency of an interface matters and there is an automated CI/CD pipeline. It complements the classic functional tests, but it requires the careful management of the references (updating them on deliberate changes of the design, for example).","const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');\\ntest('homepage screenshot', async ({ page }) => {\\n  await page.goto('https://example.com');\\n  await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot('homepage.png');\\n});"],[964,"ESLint is a tool for the static analysis of JavaScript code. It finds syntactic errors, discrepancies with the code style and potential bugs. ESLint's rules are configured for the project, which helps a team observe a single code style. It is run both by hand and automatically before a commit or in CI/CD.","What is ESLint and what is it for?\\nESLint is a static code analyser for JavaScript (and TypeScript through plugins). It checks source code for conformity to the rules given without executing it. The main aim is to reveal errors, discrepancies of style and potentially problematic patterns automatically. That helps maintain the cleanliness and the uniformity of a code base, especially in large teams.\\nHow does it work?\\nESLint analyses the code, builds an abstract syntax tree (AST) and applies a set of rules to it. Every rule is a function that checks particular nodes of the AST. If a rule finds a violation, ESLint reports an error or a warning.\\nAn example of the configuration and use\\nThe installation and the basic configuration:\\n\\nAn example of the configuration file \`.eslintrc.json\`:\\n\\nAn example of code with an error:\\n\\nESLint will give the warning: \`Missing semicolon (semi)\`.\\nWhere is it applied?\\nESLint is used in any JavaScript/TypeScript project. It is integrated into code editors (the highlighting of errors on the fly), into pre-commit hooks (through husky/lint-staged) and into CI/CD pipelines for an automatic check before a merge.\\nConclusion: ESLint is a mandatory tool for maintaining the quality of code in modern JS projects. It is worth applying always, when there is more than one developer on a project or when the stability and the readability of the code matter.","npm install eslint --save-dev\\nnpx eslint --init\\n\\n{\\n  \\"env\\": {\\n    \\"browser\\": true,\\n    \\"es2021\\": true\\n  },\\n  \\"extends\\": \\"eslint:recommended\\",\\n  \\"rules\\": {\\n    \\"no-unused-vars\\": \\"warn\\",\\n    \\"semi\\": [\\"error\\", \\"always\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nlet x = 5\\nconsole.log(x)"],[965,"To debug a frontend application, the browser's built-in developer tools (DevTools) are used. The main capabilities: viewing and changing HTML/CSS, setting breakpoints in JavaScript code, analysing network requests, profiling performance. DevTools can be opened with the F12 key or through the context menu.","The basics of debugging in the browser\\nThe developer tools (DevTools) are a set of means built into the browser, which let you analyse and debug frontend applications. They are available in every modern browser (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari) and are opened with the F12 key or through the context menu.\\nThe key capabilities of DevTools\\n\\n- Elements (the Inspector) — viewing and editing the HTML structure and the CSS styles in real time. It lets you experiment with the layout without reloading the page.\\n- Console — the printing of errors, warnings and logs. You can carry out arbitrary JavaScript code to check hypotheses.\\n- Sources — viewing the source code, setting breakpoints, the step-by-step execution of code, viewing the values of variables.\\n- Network — the analysis of every network request: the loading time, the headers, the body of the response. It helps you diagnose problems with an API or with the loading of resources.\\n- Performance — the recording and analysis of a page's performance: the loading, the rendering, the execution of the scripts.\\nAn example of the use of breakpoints\\n\\nWork with source maps\\nIf the code is minified or transpiled (from TypeScript or React, for example), source maps let you see the original source code rather than the compiled one. For that, the generation of source maps has to be switched on in the bundler (Webpack, Vite).\\nConclusion\\nDevTools are an indispensable tool for every frontend developer. They let you find and correct errors in the layout, the logic and the performance quickly without leaving the browser.","// The source code of the function that has to be debugged\\nfunction calculateTotal(price, tax) {\\n  const taxAmount = price * tax;\\n  const total = price + taxAmount;\\n  return total;\\n}\\n\\n// In the DevTools Sources tab we find this file,\\n// and click on the number of the line with const total = ... in order to set a breakpoint.\\n// When the function is called the execution will stop at that line,\\n// and you can see the values of price, tax and taxAmount in the Scope panel."],[966,"JavaScript is a language that is interpreted and compiled at the same time. Modern engines such as V8 use an intermediate stage of JIT (just-in-time) compilation in order to turn JavaScript into machine code before execution. Interpretation lets you start code quickly, but thanks to JIT compilation the performance rises, which makes JavaScript fast and effective to execute.","JavaScript was originally considered an interpreted language, where the code is read and carried out line by line at runtime. Modern JavaScript engines such as V8 (used in Google Chrome and Node.js) combine interpretation with JIT (just-in-time) compilation, however, which raises performance.\\nWhen JavaScript is carried out, the engine first interprets the code, turning it into bytecode that can be carried out quickly. Then, if the code is carried out many times (in a loop, for example), the engine uses JIT compilation in order to turn that bytecode into optimised machine code. That lets JavaScript be both flexible (like interpreted languages) and fast (like compiled ones).\\nFor example, if you have the following code:\\n\\nOn the first execution of \`calculate\` the engine interprets the function. But if the function is called many times, the engine will compile it by means of JIT in order to make its execution faster.\\nThis hybrid model makes JavaScript one of the most performant languages for web development.","function calculate(a, b) {\\n  return a + b; \\n} \\nfor (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\\n  calculate(i, i + 1); \\n}"],[967,"PUT replaces a resource entirely, sending all of its fields even if only one changes. PATCH applies a partial update, changing only the fields passed. PUT is idempotent — a repeated request gives the same result. PATCH is not necessarily idempotent, if the changes accumulate.","The main difference between PATCH and PUT\\nIn REST architecture PUT and PATCH are used to update resources, but they work differently. PUT replaces the whole resource in its entirety: the client sends a complete representation of the object, and the server saves it as it is. PATCH, for its part, applies partial changes — the client passes only those fields that have to be updated, or an instruction for the modification.\\nIdempotency\\nPUT is idempotent: the repeated calling of one and the same PUT request leads to a single state of the resource. PATCH can be non-idempotent, if the changes accumulate (increase the counter by 1, for example).\\nExamples of code\\nLet us look at a user resource:\\n\\nWhen to use what\\n\\n- Use PUT when the client sends the complete state of the resource and wants to replace it.\\n- Use PATCH for partial updates, in order to reduce the volume of data transferred and avoid overwriting the fields that have not changed.\\nConclusion: PUT suits the complete replacement of a resource, PATCH suits pinpoint changes. The choice of method affects performance, idempotency and the semantics of the API.","// The original resource\\n{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\",\\n  \\"email\\": \\"alice@example.com\\",\\n  \\"age\\": 30\\n}\\n\\n// PUT — a complete replacement\\nPUT /users/1\\n{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\",\\n  \\"email\\": \\"alice@new.com\\",\\n  \\"age\\": 31\\n}\\n// The result: every field has been replaced\\n\\n// PATCH — a partial update\\nPATCH /users/1\\n{\\n  \\"email\\": \\"alice@new.com\\"\\n}\\n// The result: only the email has been changed, the remaining fields have been preserved"],[968,"SSR (Server-Side Rendering) generates the HTML on the server and sends the finished page to the client. CSR (Client-Side Rendering) sends empty HTML and JavaScript, which renders the page in the browser. SSR improves SEO and the time of the first load, but it loads the server. CSR is faster after the loading and reduces the load on the server, but it is worse for SEO.","The main difference between SSR and CSR\\nSSR (Server-Side Rendering) and CSR (Client-Side Rendering) are two approaches to rendering web pages. In SSR the HTML is generated on the server for every request, while in CSR it is generated on the client by means of JavaScript after the page has loaded.\\nHow SSR works\\nWith SSR the server receives the request, carries out the logic necessary (queries to the database, for example), forms a complete HTML document and sends it to the browser. The browser displays the finished page at once. An example in Node.js with Express:\\n\\nHow CSR works\\nWith CSR the server sends a minimal HTML file with a link to the JavaScript. The browser loads the JS, which then renders the content. An example with React:\\n\\nWhen to use what\\n\\n- SSR — for sites with high requirements as regards SEO (online shops, blogs, for example) and to improve the time of the first load (the First Contentful Paint).\\n- CSR — for applications with a rich interface (dashboards, admin panels, for example), where interactivity after the loading matters.\\nConclusion: SSR is better for content sites, CSR for dynamic applications. A hybrid approach (Next.js, for example) is often used in order to obtain the advantages of both.","const express = require('express');\\nconst app = express();\\napp.get('/', (req, res) => {\\n  const html = \`<html><body><h1>Hello, SSR!</h1></body></html>\`;\\n  res.send(html);\\n});\\napp.listen(3000);\\n\\n// index.html\\n<html><body><div id=\\"root\\"></div><script src=\\"app.js\\"><\/script></body></html>\\n\\n// app.js\\nimport React from 'react';\\nimport ReactDOM from 'react-dom';\\nReactDOM.render(<h1>Hello, CSR!</h1>, document.getElementById('root'));"],[969,"Yes, technically it is possible, but it breaks the semantics of REST. PATCH is intended for the partial update of a resource, while PUT is intended for its complete replacement. The use of PATCH for a complete replacement can mislead other developers and break the API's contract.","What are PATCH and PUT?\\nIn a RESTful API the HTTP methods determine the type of operation on a resource. PUT is used for the complete replacement of a resource: the client sends a complete representation of the resource, and the server replaces the existing resource with that representation. PATCH is intended for a partial update: the client sends only those fields that have to be changed, and the server applies those changes to the existing resource.\\nCan a resource be replaced through PATCH?\\nTechnically, yes. If you send a PATCH request with the complete body of the resource, the server can handle it as a complete replacement. That contradicts the semantics of the HTTP protocol and the principles of REST, however. Other developers reading the code will expect PATCH to change only part of the resource and PUT to replace it entirely. Breaking this convention can lead to errors and confusion.\\nExamples of code\\nLet us look at a user resource:\\n\\nIf we were to use PATCH for a complete replacement, it would look like this:\\n\\nConclusion\\nAlthough it is technically possible to replace a resource through PATCH, it is not recommended. Follow the standards of REST: use PUT for a complete replacement and PATCH for a partial update. That makes an API predictable and understandable to everyone taking part in the development.","// The original resource\\n{\\n  \\"id\\": 1,\\n  \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\",\\n  \\"email\\": \\"alice@example.com\\"\\n}\\n\\n// PUT — a complete replacement\\nPUT /users/1\\nBody: {\\"name\\": \\"Bob\\", \\"email\\": \\"bob@example.com\\"}\\n// The result: the resource has been replaced entirely\\n\\n// PATCH — a partial update\\nPATCH /users/1\\nBody: {\\"email\\": \\"alice@newdomain.com\\"}\\n// The result: only the email has been changed, name stays \\"Alice\\"\\n\\nPATCH /users/1\\nBody: {\\"name\\": \\"Bob\\", \\"email\\": \\"bob@example.com\\"}\\n// The result: the resource has been replaced, but the semantics has been broken"],[970,"The bubbling phase is the phase of the propagation of an event in the DOM, when the event, after the target element, rises up the tree to the root. That lets you handle events on the parent elements. A click on a button bubbles up to the body, for example. An understanding of this phase helps you attach handlers correctly and avoid unexpected behaviour.","What is the bubbling phase?\\nThe bubbling phase is the second phase of the propagation of an event in the DOM, after the capturing phase. When an event happens on the target element, it starts to bubble up the DOM hierarchy: from the target element to its parent, then to the parent's parent and so on as far as the root itself (document). It is the standard behaviour for most events (click, mousedown, for example).\\nHow does it work?\\nImagine the structure: div > p > button. On a click on the button the event first passes through the capturing phase (from document to button), then reaches the target (the target phase), and after that the bubbling begins: button -> p -> div -> body -> html -> document. At every stage you can intercept the event by means of a handler, if one has been assigned on the corresponding element.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nBubbling is used for event delegation: instead of assigning a handler to every child element, you can assign a single handler on the parent and catch the events from the children through event.target. That improves performance and makes the code simpler, especially with the dynamic addition of elements.\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of the bubbling phase is necessary for working effectively with events in JavaScript, especially when implementing delegation and when debugging the unexpected firing of handlers.","document.querySelector('div').addEventListener('click', () => {\\n  console.log('div clicked');\\n});\\ndocument.querySelector('p').addEventListener('click', () => {\\n  console.log('p clicked');\\n});\\ndocument.querySelector('button').addEventListener('click', () => {\\n  console.log('button clicked');\\n});\\n// On a click on the button the output will be:\\n// button clicked\\n// p clicked\\n// div clicked"],[971,"Tokens are often held in cookies because that lets them be sent automatically with every HTTP request to the server. Cookies can be marked as httpOnly, which protects them from access through JavaScript and reduces the risk of XSS attacks. You can also set the Secure (HTTPS only) and SameSite (protection against CSRF) flags. It is a convenient and relatively secure way of managing sessions.","Why are cookies popular for holding tokens?\\nCookies are small fragments of data that the browser holds and sends automatically to the server with every request to the domain that set them. That makes them a natural choice for holding authentication tokens, since the developer does not have to add the token to the headers of every request by hand.\\nThe key advantages\\n\\n- Automatic sending: The browser itself adds the cookie to the \`Cookie\` header on every request, which makes the client code simpler.\\n- The httpOnly flag: If you set \`httpOnly\`, the cookie becomes inaccessible to JavaScript (\`document.cookie\`). That protects the token from theft through XSS attacks.\\n- The Secure flag: Restricts the sending of the cookie to HTTPS only, preventing interception in unprotected networks.\\n- The SameSite attribute: Helps protect against CSRF attacks by restricting the sending of the cookie with cross-site requests.\\nAn example of setting a cookie on the server (Node.js with Express)\\n\\nThe shortcomings and the alternatives\\nCookies have a restriction on size (about 4 KB) and can be vulnerable to CSRF if \`SameSite\` is not configured. An alternative is to hold the token in \`localStorage\` and send it through the \`Authorization\` header, but that is less secure with XSS, since JavaScript has direct access to the store.\\nConclusion: Holding tokens in cookies with the httpOnly, Secure and SameSite flags is standard practice for web applications requiring a balance between convenience and security. It is especially useful for server applications, where access to the token from JavaScript is not needed.","const express = require('express');\\nconst app = express();\\n\\napp.post('/login', (req, res) => {\\n  const token = 'some-jwt-token';\\n  res.cookie('auth_token', token, {\\n    httpOnly: true,\\n    secure: true,\\n    sameSite: 'strict',\\n    maxAge: 3600000 // 1 hour\\n  });\\n  res.send('Logged in');\\n});\\n\\napp.get('/profile', (req, res) => {\\n  const token = req.cookies.auth_token;\\n  if (!token) return res.status(401).send('Unauthorized');\\n  // the checking of the token\\n  res.send('Profile data');\\n});"],[972,"Generator functions in Redux Saga are used to manage asynchronous operations such as requests to an API. They let you suspend and resume the execution, which makes the code more readable and testable. The sagas use yield to wait for effects, and the middleware handles them. That makes working with side effects in Redux simpler.","What are generators needed for in Redux Saga?\\nRedux Saga is middleware for managing side effects in Redux applications. Generator functions are the foundation of Saga, since they let you write asynchronous code in a synchronous style. Generators can suspend their execution by means of the \`yield\` keyword and resume it later, which is ideally suited to handling sequential asynchronous actions.\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen a saga is carried out, it hands control over to the middleware through \`yield\`. The middleware handles the effect (a call to an API, for example) and returns the result back into the generator. That makes the code linear and easily readable, without callbacks or promises.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nIn this example \`call\` suspends the saga until the call to the API has finished, while \`put\` sends an action into the Redux store.\\nThe advantages of using generators\\n\\n- Making testing simpler: you can check the effects without carrying out real requests.\\n- Readability: the code looks synchronous, which makes the logic easier to understand.\\n- The management of complex flows: it is easy to handle parallel tasks, cancellations and delays.\\nConclusion: Generator functions in Redux Saga are applied for the effective management of asynchronous operations, making the code predictable and testable. That is especially useful in large applications with a multitude of side effects.","import { call, put, takeEvery } from 'redux-saga/effects';\\nimport { fetchUserApi } from './api';\\n\\nfunction* fetchUser(action) {\\n  try {\\n    const user = yield call(fetchUserApi, action.payload);\\n    yield put({ type: 'FETCH_USER_SUCCESS', payload: user });\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    yield put({ type: 'FETCH_USER_FAILURE', error });\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction* watchFetchUser() {\\n  yield takeEvery('FETCH_USER_REQUEST', fetchUser);\\n}"],[973,"A library of components is a set of reusable UI elements gathered into a separate package. It is usually held in a monorepository or a separate repository, published to an internal npm registry and documented by means of Storybook. Every component should be isolated, covered by tests and versioned. That speeds up development and maintains the visual uniformity of the products.","What a library of components is\\nA library of components is a centralised set of reusable UI elements (buttons, inputs, modal windows and so on) that are used in every one of a company's projects. It is the technical implementation of a design system and lets developers avoid creating the same elements from scratch every time.\\nThe organisation of the holding and the versioning\\nMost often the library is placed in a separate repository or in a monorepository (using Nx or Turborepo, for example). Every component is a separate module with its own styles, tests and documentation. The versioning happens by semver, and the publication to an internal npm registry (Verdaccio, GitHub Packages or JFrog).\\nThe process of developing a component\\nThe development is carried out in isolation by means of tools of the Storybook kind, which lets you view and test components in different states. Every component should:\\n\\n- Take its settings through props (customisation)\\n- Be accessible (a11y)\\n- Have unit tests and visual regression tests (Chromatic, for example)\\n- Contain documentation with examples of use\\nAn example of the structure of a component\\n\\nThe integration into projects\\nProjects connect the library as an ordinary npm dependency. When the version of the library is updated, developers receive the new components and the corrections. For backward compatibility it matters to follow semver and to publish a changelog.\\nConclusion: An internal library of components is justified when a company has several products or teams and a single visual style is required. It reduces the duplication of code, speeds up development and makes maintaining the UI simpler.","// Button/Button.tsx\\nimport React from 'react';\\nimport './Button.css';\\n\\ninterface ButtonProps {\\n  variant: 'primary' | 'secondary';\\n  children: React.ReactNode;\\n  onClick?: () => void;\\n}\\n\\nexport const Button: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({ variant, children, onClick }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <button className={\`btn btn--\${variant}\`} onClick={onClick}>\\n      {children}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n};"],[974,"To compare two arrays by the number of identical elements you have to count the frequency of every element in both arrays and then compare those frequencies. You can use an object or a Map to hold the counters. For example, create a count object for the first array, then decrease the counters for the second array and check that every value is equal to zero.","The comparison of arrays by the number of identical elements\\nThis task arises when you have to check whether two arrays contain the same elements with the same frequency, regardless of the order. In testing or when working with sets of data, for example.\\nThe main approach\\nThe counting of the frequency of the elements by means of an object or a Map is used. First a counter is created for the first array, then the counter is decreased for the second array. If at the end every value of the counter is equal to zero, the arrays are equivalent as regards the number of elements.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nApplication\\nThis method is used in algorithms for checking anagrams, in testing for comparing results, and also in the analysis of data for checking the distribution of values.\\nConclusion: The comparison of arrays by the frequency of the elements is an effective way of checking the equivalence of sets of data, when the order does not matter. Apply it for tasks connected with counting matches or checking the identity of content.","function compareArraysByFrequency(arr1, arr2) {\\n  if (arr1.length !== arr2.length) return false;\\n  const count = {};\\n  for (let item of arr1) {\\n    count[item] = (count[item] || 0) + 1;\\n  }\\n  for (let item of arr2) {\\n    if (!count[item]) return false;\\n    count[item]--;\\n  }\\n  return true;\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(compareArraysByFrequency([1,2,2,3], [2,1,3,2])); // true\\nconsole.log(compareArraysByFrequency([1,2], [1,2,2])); // false"],[975,"The main attributes of cookies: Name, Value, Domain, Path, Expires/Max-Age, Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite. Name and Value set the name and the value. Domain and Path determine for which URLs the cookie is sent. Expires or Max-Age set the lifetime. Secure requires HTTPS. HttpOnly forbids access through JavaScript. SameSite restricts the sending in cross-site requests.","The main attributes of cookies\\nCookies are small fragments of data that the server sends to the browser, and the browser holds them and sends them back with every request to the server. The attributes of cookies control their behaviour, their security and their period of validity.\\nThe list of attributes\\n\\n- Name — the name of the cookie (mandatory).\\n- Value — the value of the cookie (mandatory).\\n- Domain — the domain for which the cookie is valid (example.com, for example).\\n- Path — the URL path for which the cookie is sent (/, for example).\\n- Expires — the date of the expiry of the period of validity (in the HTTP-date format).\\n- Max-Age — the lifetime in seconds (it takes priority over Expires).\\n- Secure — a flag stating that the cookie has to be transferred only over HTTPS.\\n- HttpOnly — a flag forbidding access to the cookie through JavaScript (document.cookie).\\n- SameSite — restricts the sending of the cookie in cross-site requests (Strict, Lax, None).\\nAn example of setting a cookie on the server (Node.js with Express)\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe attributes of cookies let you configure their behaviour finely: from restricting the area of validity to raising security. Use Secure and HttpOnly for protection against interception and XSS attacks, and SameSite to prevent CSRF attacks.","res.cookie('sessionId', 'abc123', {\\n  domain: '.example.com',\\n  path: '/',\\n  maxAge: 3600000, // 1 hour\\n  secure: true,\\n  httpOnly: true,\\n  sameSite: 'strict'\\n});"],[976,"GET requests are cached because they are considered idempotent and safe — they do not change the state of the server. Caching lets the browser and intermediate proxies save the responses to repeated GET requests, which reduces the load on the server and speeds up the loading of pages for the user.","Why are GET requests cached?\\nGET requests are idempotent and safe by the definition of the HTTP specification. That means that carrying out one and the same GET request many times should not change the state of the server and should always return the same result (with the data unchanged). It is precisely this property that makes GET requests ideal candidates for caching.\\nHow does the caching of GET requests work?\\nWhen a browser or a proxy server receives a response to a GET request, it can save it in the local cache. On a repeated request to the same URL the system first checks the cache. If the response is there and it has not gone stale (according to the Cache-Control, Expires and other headers), then the request is not sent to the server and the saved response is used.\\nAn example of code with caching headers\\n\\nPractical application\\n\\n- Static resources (CSS, JS, images) — they are cached to speed up the loading of pages.\\n- API endpoints returning immutable data (a list of countries, for example).\\n- The reduction of the load on the server and of the latency for the user.\\nConclusion\\nThe caching of GET requests is a key mechanism for optimising web applications, which should be applied for every idempotent and safe request, especially for static content and rarely changing data.","// Server code in Node.js with Express\\napp.get('/api/data', (req, res) => {\\n  res.set('Cache-Control', 'public, max-age=3600'); // cache for 1 hour\\n  res.json({ message: 'This response is cached' });\\n});"],[977,"Immutability means that an object or a value cannot be changed after it has been created. Mutability, by contrast, lets data be changed. In JavaScript the primitive data types (strings, numbers, for example) are immutable, while objects and arrays can be changed. Immutable data structures let you avoid unexpected changes and make programs more predictable.","Immutability is the concept by which data cannot be changed after it has been created. Instead of changing the original data, a new copy with the changes brought in is created. In JavaScript the primitive types, such as strings, numbers and boolean values, are immutable, which means that any operations on them create new values. For example:\\n\\nMutable objects, such as arrays and objects, can be changed. That means you can change their state directly:\\n\\nImmutability is especially important in the context of React and other frameworks that rely on immutable data structures to track changes of state. When working with state (in React, for example), changing the state directly can lead to unpredictable results, whereas the use of methods that return new objects (\`concat\` instead of \`push\` for arrays, for example) helps you avoid side effects.\\nAn example of the use of immutability in React:\\n\\nImmutability improves performance (it lets changes be tracked more easily) and prevents unexpected changes of data in different parts of a program, which makes code more predictable and safer.","let str = \\"Hello\\"; \\nstr[0] = \\"h\\"; // Strings are immutable, this operation will not change the string \\nconsole.log(str); // \\"Hello\\"\\n\\nlet arr = [1, 2, 3]; \\narr[0] = 10; \\nconsole.log(arr); // [10, 2, 3]\\n\\nconst newState = [...oldState, newItem]; // We create a new array instead of changing the old one"],[978,"A refresh token is intended for obtaining a new access token rather than for the authentication of every request. Sending a refresh token with every request increases the risk of its interception and of the compromising of the account. An access token, by contrast, has a short lifetime and is used for every request, which minimises the consequences of a leak. A refresh token is held in a safe place (an httpOnly cookie, for example) and is passed only when the token is refreshed.","The separation of the responsibility of the tokens\\nIn modern authentication systems two types of token are used: an access token and a refresh token. An access token is a short-lived token (usually 15-30 minutes), which is sent with every request to protected resources. A refresh token is a long-lived token (days or weeks), which is used only to obtain a new access token when the old one expires.\\nWhy it is not worth sending a refresh token with every request\\n\\n- Security: If a refresh token is intercepted by an attacker, they will be able to obtain new access tokens for an unlimited time, until the refresh token is revoked. Sending it with every request increases the attack surface many times over.\\n- Performance: The checking of a refresh token requires additional resources (a query to the database or a check in a store, for example), which slows down the handling of every request.\\n- Architectural cleanliness: An access token should be self-sufficient (a signed JWT, for example), so that the server can check it without turning to the database. A refresh token, on the other hand, requires a check on the server's side, which makes it unsuitable for frequent sending.\\nA practical example\\n\\nConclusion\\nA refresh token should be used only to refresh an access token, not to authenticate every request. That raises security, reduces the load on the server and conforms to the best practices of designing authentication systems.","// The client sends the access token with every request\\nfetch('/api/data', {\\n  headers: {\\n    'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + accessToken\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// Only when the access token expires does the client use the refresh token\\nasync function refreshAccessToken() {\\n  const response = await fetch('/api/auth/refresh', {\\n    method: 'POST',\\n    credentials: 'include', // the httpOnly cookie with the refresh token\\n  });\\n  const data = await response.json();\\n  accessToken = data.accessToken;\\n}"],[979,"Asynchronous generators in JavaScript let you generate values with the ability to wait for asynchronous operations to finish. They work using \`async\` and \`yield\`, which makes them useful for creating streams of data that arrive gradually. That is convenient when working with large sets of data or with external sources such as an API, where the data can arrive in parts.","Asynchronous generators are generators that can manage asynchronous operations inside themselves, using \`async\` and \`await\`. They let you return values not at once but gradually, as the asynchronous operations are carried out. That is especially useful when you have to work with streams of data that arrive in parts or are handled as they arrive — data from an API or work with files, for example.\\nAn asynchronous generator is defined by means of the \`async function*\` keyword, and to obtain the data from it \`for-await-of\` is used. An example:\\n\\nIn this example the asynchronous generator returns values at intervals of time. It is useful when you are handling data that arrives as it becomes ready (the reading of files or of data from a server, for example), letting you write code that looks synchronous but handles asynchronous processes.","async function* asyncGenerator() {\\n  yield new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve(\\"First\\"), 1000));  \\n  yield new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve(\\"Second\\"), 2000));  \\n  yield new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve(\\"Third\\"), 3000)); \\n} \\n(async () => {\\n  for await (let value of asyncGenerator()) {\\n    console.log(value);  \\n  } \\n})();"],[980,"Asynchronous iteration using \`for-await-of\` lets you work with asynchronous iterators, such as asynchronous generators or promises, which return data gradually. The \`for-await-of\` operator waits for every promise to resolve before moving to the next iteration. That is convenient for handling streams of data or sequential asynchronous operations.","Asynchronous iteration was introduced in ES2018 and lets you iterate over collections of data that arrive asynchronously. It is usually used with asynchronous generators, which return promises. In a \`for-await-of\` loop every promise returned by the iterator is resolved before the code moves on to the next value.\\nAn example of use:\\n\\n\`for-await-of\` blocks every iteration until the current promise has resolved, which makes it useful for working with streams of data or with sequential asynchronous tasks. That makes the handling of data that arrives as it becomes ready simpler — data from an API or the reading of files, for example.","async function* asyncGenerator() {\\n  yield new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve(\\"Data 1\\"), 1000));  \\n  yield new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve(\\"Data 2\\"), 1000)); \\n} \\n(async () => {\\n  for await (let value of asyncGenerator()) {\\n    console.log(value); // Will print \\"Data 1\\" and \\"Data 2\\" with a delay  \\n  } \\n})();"],[981,"The Reflect API is a built-in object in JavaScript that provides methods for working with objects at a lower level, similar to the methods available through the \`Object\` object but with more predictable and transparent behaviour. It lets you easily manage objects, call methods, define properties, and also intercept actions on objects using a Proxy. That is convenient for improving control over objects and for working with metaprogramming.","The Reflect API was added in ES6 and is a set of methods for working with objects, which provide the means for managing objects and their properties. That includes methods for calling functions, creating new objects, setting and obtaining the values of properties and other operations, which help make code more predictable and convenient to read.\\nThe methods of Reflect are similar to the methods available through \`Object\`, but they return values or errors in a more predictable form. Reflect makes the process of setting the properties of objects simpler and more controllable, for example.\\nAn example of the use of Reflect:\\n\\nReflect is also useful when working with a Proxy, in order to intercept operations on objects carefully. By means of it you can control various actions, such as the reading or writing of an object's properties, and manage the behaviour of objects at the level of metaprogramming. It is often used, for example, to create flexible, controllable APIs where it matters to track or change the interaction with objects.","let user = {  name: \\"John\\",  age: 30 }; \\n// Setting a new property through Reflect \\nReflect.set(user, 'age', 31); \\nconsole.log(user.age); // 31 \\n\\n// Calling a function through Reflect \\nlet result = Reflect.apply(Math.max, Math, [1, 2, 3]); \\nconsole.log(result); // 3"],[982,"Private fields in classes, denoted by the \`#\` symbol, are accessible only inside the class itself, which lets you hide them from outside code. That helps protect an object's data from accidental change or access. Private fields cannot be changed or read directly outside the class, which makes them a safer way of holding an object's state.","Private fields are a new mechanism in JavaScript, which appeared in ES2020 and lets you hide data inside classes from outside access. Such fields begin with the \`#\` symbol, and they are accessible only inside the methods of that class. That is useful when you have to protect an object's data and be sure it will not be accidentally changed or used in an incorrect context.\\nAn example of use:\\n\\nPrivate fields add strict encapsulation to JavaScript, which prevents access to objects' internal data. That is useful for creating secure APIs, where an object's state should not be directly changed or accessible from outside. The main advantage consists in the protection of the data and the improvement of the structuring of the code.","class User {\\n    #password;    \\n    constructor(name, password) {\\n          this.name = name;    \\n          this.#password = password;  \\n    }    \\n    checkPassword(inputPassword) {\\n          return this.#password === inputPassword;  \\n    } \\n} \\nconst user = new User('John', 'secret'); \\n\\nconsole.log(user.#password); // An error — access to a private field is impossible \\nconsole.log(user.checkPassword('secret')); // true"],[983,"A closure is a function that remembers its surroundings. The problem arises when, in loops, the closures \\"remember\\" one and the same value of a variable that changes on every iteration. That leads to every closure using one and the same final value. To avoid this problem you can use \`let\` instead of \`var\`, or create a new scope by means of an IIFE.","A closure is a function that \\"remembers\\" the variables from its lexical surroundings, even if that function is carried out outside its original context. The problem with closures in loops arises when a closure \\"grabs\\" a variable that changes on every iteration, but by the time every closure is carried out that variable already has its final value.\\nAn example with the problem:\\n\\nThat happens because the closure in every call of \`setTimeout\` uses one and the same value of \`i\`, which has already changed by the moment of execution.\\nThe solution to the problem is the use of \`let\`, which creates a new scope for every iteration of the loop:\\n\\nAnother way is to use an immediately invoked function expression (IIFE), which will create a new scope:\\n\\nThese methods prevent the problem by ensuring the preservation of a unique value for every iteration, which matters for the correct working of closures in loops.","for (var i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {\\n    setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 1000); \\n} \\n// Will print 4 three times instead of 1, 2, 3\\n\\nfor (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {\\n    setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 1000); // 1, 2, 3 \\n}\\n\\nfor (var i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {\\n    (function(i) {\\n          setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 1000);  \\n    })(i); \\n}"],[984,"The garbage collector in JavaScript automatically frees memory by deleting the objects to which there are no longer any references. The main mechanism is \\"mark-and-sweep\\", where objects are marked as reachable or not. The optimisation of work with memory consists in avoiding holding on to unnecessary references to objects and remembering closures, which can keep data in memory for longer than is required.","In JavaScript the management of memory happens automatically by means of the garbage collector. The garbage collector looks for objects in memory that are no longer used and frees the memory occupied by those objects. The main algorithm used in most modern JavaScript engines is called \\"mark-and-sweep\\".\\nThe algorithm works as follows: the garbage collector begins with the root objects (usually the global variables and the objects connected with the current execution of the program), marks them as \\"reachable\\", and then goes over every reference from those objects to other objects. If an object is unreachable from any chain of references, it is considered rubbish and its memory is freed.\\nAn example of a potential memory leak:\\n\\nIn this example the closure preserves a reference to the array even if it is no longer needed, which can lead to a memory leak.\\nTo minimise problems with memory, it matters to:\\n\\n- Delete the references to objects when they are no longer needed.\\n\\n- Avoid long-lived closures if they hold references to large objects.\\n\\n- Unsubscribe from events in good time, so that the handlers do not continue holding references to objects.\\n\\nThat lets you avoid superfluous expenditure of memory and improve a program's performance.","function createClosure() {\\n    let largeArray = new Array(1000000);  \\n    return function() {\\n          console.log(largeArray.length);  \\n   }; \\n} \\nconst closure = createClosure();"],[985,"Event delegation is the technique by which an event is handled on the parent element rather than on the specific child elements. That works thanks to the bubbling of events in JavaScript, where an event happening on an element rises up the DOM tree to its ancestors. Event delegation improves performance, since it lets you minimise the number of handlers on a page, especially if the elements are created dynamically.","Event delegation uses the ability of events to \\"bubble\\" up the DOM tree, handing the control of the event handler over from a specific element to its parent element or even to a higher level in the DOM. In the typical scenario every interactive element can be assigned its own event handler, which can lead to a worsening of performance if there are many such elements. With event delegation, however, we can assign a single handler on the parent element, and it will handle the events of all of its child elements.\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn this example the parent element \`parent\` will handle the clicks of every button inside it.\\nThat is far more effective than attaching a handler to every button separately.\\nEvent delegation is especially useful in dynamically changing applications, where elements can be added or removed after the page has loaded. That improves performance, since the handlers do not have to be recreated for the new elements every time, and the overall number of handlers on the page is reduced.","document.getElementById('parent').addEventListener('click', (event) => {\\n    if (event.target.tagName === 'BUTTON') {\\n          console.log('Button clicked:', event.target.textContent);  \\n    } \\n});"],[986,"Memoisation is a technique that lets you save the results of the execution of functions for reuse, so as not to compute them again if the input data has not changed. That is especially useful for heavy computations or for functions that are often called with the same parameters. Memoisation helps reduce the load on the processor, improving an application's performance.","Memoisation is the process of caching the results of a function depending on its arguments. When the function is called again with the same arguments, the saved result is returned instead of the computations being carried out again. That is especially useful for functions that carry out complex or long computations.\\nAn example of the simplest memoisation:\\n\\nIn this example the \`slowFunction\` function computes a value on the basis of the input argument. By means of memoisation we save the result of the first call and return it on the subsequent calls with the same argument, avoiding the repeated carrying out of the computations.\\nMemoisation is often used in cases where a function is called several times with the same parameters — when working with React components, with complex computations or with requests to an API, for example. That can substantially improve performance by reducing the number of unnecessary operations.","function memoize(fn) {\\n    const cache = {};  \\n    return function (...args) {\\n          const key = JSON.stringify(args);    \\n          if (cache[key]) {\\n                return cache[key];    \\n          }    \\n          const result = fn(...args);    \\n          cache[key] = result;    \\n          return result;  \\n    }; \\n} \\n\\nconst slowFunction = (num) => {\\n  console.log('Computing...');  \\n  return num * 2; \\n}; \\n\\nconst memoizedFunction = memoize(slowFunction); \\nconsole.log(memoizedFunction(5)); // \\"Computing...\\" 10 \\nconsole.log(memoizedFunction(5)); // 10 (the result has been taken from the cache)"],[987,"Asynchronous rendering lets the browser carry out updates of the user interface in parallel with other tasks, without blocking the main thread. In modern JavaScript, asynchronous tasks such as requests to a server are carried out in the background, and the updates of the interface happen only after those tasks have finished. That lets you improve the responsiveness of the interface and avoid it \\"freezing\\" while heavy operations are carried out.","Asynchronous rendering and updating in JavaScript depend on how the language works with events and tasks. JavaScript uses a single-threaded model of execution, but it can carry out asynchronous operations such as requests to a server or timers in parallel thanks to the event loop mechanism.\\nAsynchronous rendering is the process by which the browser breaks the execution of tasks up into small parts and updates the interface between them. That lets you carry out complex computations or work with data without blocking the interface. Asynchronous tasks are carried out in the background, for example, and when they finish (the requesting of data, for example) they are added to the task queue and carried out at the next iteration of the event loop.\\nAsynchronous updates usually happen through methods such as \`setTimeout\`, \`Promise\`, \`async/await\` or APIs for requests to a server (\`fetch\`, for example):\\n\\nIn this example the asynchronous function \`fetchData\` loads data from a server. While the data is loading, the interface stays responsive, since the request is carried out asynchronously.\\nAsynchronous rendering and updating matter for creating a smooth user experience. They let the browser handle heavy tasks without blocking the interaction with the user, and update the interface only once every operation needed has finished. That is especially relevant for complex web applications, where performance and responsiveness are critical.","async function fetchData() {\\n    console.log('Fetching data...');  \\n    const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data');  \\n    const data = await response.json();  \\n    console.log('Data received:', data); \\n} \\n\\nconsole.log('Before fetch'); \\nfetchData(); \\nconsole.log('After fetch');"],[988,"Static scope (or lexical scope) means that the rules determining the visibility of variables are established at the stage of writing the code rather than at runtime. That means functions remember the surroundings in which they were created. Dynamic scope, by contrast, is determined by how functions are called rather than by how they are defined, which is more characteristic of certain other languages. JavaScript uses precisely static scope.","In JavaScript scope determines where in the code particular variables can be accessed. Static (lexical) scope means that variables are available in the context in which they were defined, and that is determined at the stage of writing the code. If a function is defined inside another function, for example, then the inner function has access to the variables of the outer function, even if the outer function has already finished executing.\\nAn example of code:\\n\\nDynamic scope would be determined by how the function is called rather than by where it is defined. That is less widespread in JavaScript, since the language strictly follows static scope. It can cause confusion for developers, especially if they come from languages with dynamic scope.\\nAn understanding of static scope thus lets developers organise code better and avoid problems with access to variables.","function outerFunction() {\\n      const outerVar = 'I am from outer function';    \\n      function innerFunction() {\\n          console.log(outerVar); // Access to the outerVar variable    \\n      }    \\n      innerFunction(); \\n} \\nouterFunction(); // Output: I am from outer function"],[989,"JavaScript engines such as V8 use several optimisations to improve performance. They include compilation into machine code (JIT compilation), the optimisation of hot functions, and also the use of built-in data structures and garbage-collection mechanisms. These methods let you speed up the execution of code and reduce the delays during its execution.","JavaScript engines such as V8 are developed to maximise the performance of the execution of JavaScript code. Here are several key optimisations they use:\\n\\n- JIT compilation (Just-In-Time Compilation): Instead of interpreting JavaScript code line by line, as some other engines do, V8 compiles the code into machine code at runtime. That speeds the process up substantially, since compiled code is carried out faster than interpreted code.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n- The optimisation of hot functions: The engines track which functions are called often and apply additional optimisations to them, such as inline compilation (inlining) and the optimisation of the operations that happen inside those functions.\\n\\n- The management of memory and garbage collection: Modern engines use complex algorithms to manage memory, such as the \\"mark-and-sweep\\" algorithms, which help free the memory occupied by unneeded objects effectively, minimising memory leaks.\\n\\nThese optimisations let JavaScript programs work faster and more effectively, which matters especially in modern web applications, where performance is critical.","function add(a, b) {\\n      return a + b; \\n} \\nconsole.log(add(2, 3)); // The function can be compiled into machine code on the first call"],[990,"Inline caching is a technique used by JavaScript engines to speed up access to the properties of objects. On the first access to a property of an object type, information about its location is cached, which lets subsequent accesses avoid a repeated search. That substantially speeds up access to the properties of objects, especially in loops or in frequently called functions.","Inline caching (IC) is an optimisation strategy used in JavaScript engines such as V8 to speed up access to the properties of objects. When code accesses a property of an object, the engine carries out several actions in order to determine where that property is located.\\nOn the first access to a property, \`obj.prop\` for example, the engine does the following:\\n\\n- It finds the object and determines the location of the property.\\n\\n- It caches that location for the subsequent accesses.\\n\\nWhen the next access to the same property happens, the engine simply uses the cached location, which speeds the execution up substantially.\\nAn example of code where that can be useful:\\n\\nInline caching matters especially in the context of loops and functions that often access objects. Using this technique, the engines can reduce the execution time of the operations, which ultimately leads to applications working faster.\\nInline caching thus helps you avoid the overheads of searching for the properties of objects and improves the overall performance of JavaScript programs.","const obj = { value: 0 }; \\nfor (let i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {\\n      obj.value++; // Every access to obj.value can make use of the caching \\n}"],[991,"The deoptimisation of code is the process by which a JavaScript engine cancels the optimisations applied to functions or sections of code. That can happen because of changes in the structure of the data, the use of dynamically changing properties or the calling of functions in unusual contexts. If a function starts working with different data types, for example, the engine may return it to a slower method of execution.","The deoptimisation of code in JavaScript is the situation where optimised code is returned to a slower, unoptimised version. That happens when the engine determines that the conditions of the code's execution have changed and the previous optimisations no longer suit. Deoptimisation can cause a substantial reduction in performance, especially in highly loaded applications.\\nThe most widespread causes of deoptimisation include:\\nThe dynamic change of the structure of objects: If you change the structure of an object at runtime (add or delete properties, for example), that can lead to deoptimisation.\\n\\nThe use of different data types: When a function or a method expects a particular data type but receives another, that can also lead to deoptimisation.\\n\\nUnusual calls of functions: If functions are called in a context that differs from the one expected, that can force the engine to give up its optimisations.\\n\\nDeoptimisation can be avoided by keeping to predictable data structures and avoiding frequent changes of objects. That will let JavaScript engines optimise the code better, which will substantially raise an application's performance.","function example(obj) {\\n  obj.newProp = 1; // The addition of a property can cause deoptimisation    \\n  return obj.newProp; \\n}\\n\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n  return a + b; // If a and b change their types, deoptimisation can happen \\n}\\n\\nfunction test() {\\n  return this.value; // A call in a context different from the one expected can cause deoptimisation \\n}"],[992,"Hidden classes in V8 are internal structures the engine creates to represent the shapes of objects and their properties. When you create objects with identical properties, V8 assigns them one and the same hidden class, which speeds up access to their properties. Shape-based optimisations let the engine determine quickly where the properties are located in memory, which substantially reduces the time taken to access them.","V8 uses the concept of hidden classes to raise the performance of work with objects. When you create an object in JavaScript, V8 assigns it a hidden class, which determines the structure of the object and its properties. These hidden classes let the engine manage memory effectively and speed up access to the properties.\\nIf you create several objects with identical properties, for example, they will have the same hidden class:\\n\\nWhen V8 sees that objects have identical properties, it can use shape-based optimisation, which helps it find the location of the properties in memory quickly. If the structure of an object changes (a new property is added, for example), V8 can create a new hidden class for that changed object, which sometimes leads to the deoptimisation of the code.\\nThese optimisations let V8 speed up the execution of operations on objects substantially, reducing the overheads of access to their properties and raising the overall performance of applications.","class Person {\\n  constructor(name, age) {\\n      this.name = name;        \\n      this.age = age;    \\n  } \\n} \\nconst person1 = new Person('Alice', 30); \\nconst person2 = new Person('Bob', 25); // person1 and person2 have the same hidden class"],[993,"Just-In-Time (JIT) optimisation is the method by which JavaScript code is compiled into machine code at runtime rather than in advance. That lets the engine adapt to the real conditions of an application's work by optimising the functions and code that are called often. JIT compilation combines the advantages of interpretation and compilation, ensuring faster working of the code, especially in highly loaded applications.","Just-In-Time (JIT) optimisation is a method of compilation that lets JavaScript engines such as V8 compile code into machine code on the fly, while the application is running. That means the code can be compiled right before it is carried out, which substantially speeds up the response time and the performance.\\nThere are several stages in the working of JIT compilation:\\n\\n- The first run: When the code is carried out for the first time, it is interpreted. That lets the execution begin quickly, but it does not optimise the performance.\\n\\n- The gathering of data about the performance: During the execution the engine gathers data about which functions are called most often (the hot functions).\\n\\n- The compilation of the hot functions: Once the data has been gathered, the engine compiles those hot functions into highly efficient machine code. That lets the execution of the code be speeded up, since compiled code is carried out substantially faster than interpreted code.\\n\\nAn example of code where JIT compilation can be useful:\\n\\nJIT compilation thus combines the advantages of interpretation and compilation, ensuring faster working of the code and adaptation to changes at runtime. That is critically important for modern web applications, where performance and responsiveness play a key role.","function calculateSum(n) {\\n  let sum = 0;    \\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {     \\n    sum += i;    \\n  }    \\n  return sum; \\n} \\nconsole.log(calculateSum(1000000)); // The frequent calling of this function can cause JIT compilation"],[994,"A memory leak in JavaScript happens when a program holds on to references to objects that are no longer used, because of which the memory is not freed. That can lead to a reduction in performance and even to the application crashing. To diagnose memory leaks you can use developer tools such as the profiler in Chrome, which helps you track the use of memory and reveal the objects that are not being freed from memory.","A memory leak is the situation where a JavaScript program holds on to references to objects that are no longer needed, which prevents the garbage collector from freeing the memory. That can lead to a gradual increase in the use of memory, which can ultimately reduce an application's performance or even cause it to fail.\\nThe main causes of memory leaks include:\\n\\n- The retention of references: When objects are preserved in memory but are no longer used. If you create an array and save it in a global variable, for example, even if it is no longer needed.\\n\\n- Closures: Closures can hold on to references to outer variables even if they are no longer needed.\\n\\n- Event listeners: If event listeners are not removed, they can continue holding references to DOM elements, which causes a memory leak.\\n\\nTo diagnose memory leaks you can use the developer tools in the browser, such as Chrome DevTools. The memory profiler lets you watch the use of memory, record «snapshots» of the memory and analyse which objects are not being freed. The regular checking of the memory during development helps you reveal leaks at the early stages and preserve your application's performance.","let largeArray = new Array(1000000).fill('memory leak'); \\n// If largeArray is not reset, the memory will not be freed\\n\\nfunction createClosure() {\\n  let largeObject = { /* ... */ };    \\n  return function() {\\n    console.log(largeObject);    \\n  }; \\n} \\nlet closure = createClosure(); // largeObject will not be freed\\n\\nconst button = document.getElementById('myButton'); \\nfunction handleClick() {\\n  console.log('Button clicked'); \\n} \\nbutton.addEventListener('click', handleClick); // If the listener is not removed, the memory will not be freed"],[995,"To avoid memory leaks when using closures, you have to make sure the closures do not hold on to unnecessary references to objects. The removal of event listeners when they are no longer needed is also critical for preventing leaks. For that you can use the \`removeEventListener\` method and wrap functions in local contexts, in order to avoid the unnecessary holding of references.","To prevent memory leaks in JavaScript, especially when using closures and event listeners, several strategies should be applied.\\n\\n- The correct management of closures: Avoid creating closures that hold on to references to large objects or to variables that are no longer needed. If you have to preserve state, try to minimise the data held in the closure.\\n\\nIf you do not use \`counter\`, it will hold a reference to \`count\` in memory. It is better to use simple data structures or to pass values as parameters.\\n\\n- The removal of event listeners: Be sure to remove event listeners when they are no longer needed. That can be done by means of \`removeEventListener\`. For example:\\n\\n- The use of WeakMap and WeakSet: These data structures do not hold hard references to objects, which lets the garbage collector free the memory if there are no longer any references to the object in the program.\\n\\n- Regular checks: Periodically check your application for memory leaks by means of the developer tools. Use memory profilers to analyse the use of memory and reveal leaks.\\n\\nObserving these principles will help you minimise the likelihood of memory leaks and keep the performance of your JavaScript application at a high level.","function createCounter() {\\n  let count = 0;    \\n  return function() {\\n    count++;        \\n    return count;    \\n  }; \\n} \\nconst counter = createCounter(); // count will be held in the closure\\n\\nconst button = document.getElementById('myButton'); \\nfunction handleClick() {\\n  console.log('Button clicked'); \\n} \\nbutton.addEventListener('click', handleClick); \\n// The removal of the listener \\nbutton.removeEventListener('click', handleClick);\\n\\nconst weakMap = new WeakMap(); \\nconst obj = {}; \\nweakMap.set(obj, 'some value'); // If obj is no longer used, the memory will be freed"],[996,"Currying is the technique by which a function with several arguments is converted into a sequence of functions, each of which takes one argument. That lets you call functions partially, preserving the preceding arguments for subsequent use. Currying is useful for creating more flexible, adaptable functions that can be used in various contexts.","Currying is the concept by which a function taking several arguments is converted into a sequence of functions, each of which takes one argument. That lets you call functions conveniently in stages and preserve state for the subsequent calls.\\nAn example of currying:\\n\\nIn this example the \`add\` function takes one argument and returns another function, which takes the second argument. That lets you use the partial application of arguments.\\nCurrying is useful in various scenarios, for example:\\n\\n- The creation of specialised functions: You can create a function that will use the same initial parameters in different contexts.\\n\\n- Making functional programming simpler: Currying makes functions more modular and reusable, which matters in functional programming.\\n\\n- The improvement of the readability of code: The use of currying can make code more understandable, since you can state the order of the application of the arguments explicitly.\\n\\nCurrying can be implemented by means of standard functions or using libraries such as Lodash, which offer functions for currying and partial application. It is a powerful tool, which helps you organise and improve the structure of your code in JavaScript.","function add(a) {\\n  return function(b) {     \\n    return a + b;    \\n  }; \\n} \\nconst addFive = add(5); // A function that adds 5 \\nconsole.log(addFive(3)); // 8\\n\\nconst multiply = (a) => (b) => a * b; \\nconst double = multiply(2); \\nconsole.log(double(5)); // 10"],[997,"Lazy evaluation in JavaScript means that the result of a computation will be returned only as it is needed rather than at once. That can improve performance, especially if the results are not always needed. Lazy evaluation can be implemented by means of functions that return other functions, or by using generators, which let you suspend and resume the execution.","Lazy evaluation is the concept by which computations are postponed until the moment the result is really required. That can be useful for raising performance, especially where large data or complex computations are concerned. The main advantages of lazy evaluation include:\\nThe saving of resources: The computations are carried out only when that is necessary, which reduces the expenditure of resources.\\nThe improvement of performance: In the case of large data that can substantially shorten the execution time of a program.\\nExamples of the implementation of lazy evaluation in JavaScript:\\n\\n- The use of higher-order functions:\\n\\n- Generators: Generators let you create iterators that compute values as they are requested.\\n\\nLazy evaluation thus helps you optimise a program's working by reducing the number of unnecessary computations and improving the overall performance.","function lazyAdd(a) {\\n  return function(b) {     \\n    return a + b; // The computation happens only on the call    \\n  }; \\n} \\nconst addFive = lazyAdd(5); \\nconsole.log(addFive(10)); // 15 (the computation happened here)\\n\\nfunction* lazyNumbers() {\\n  let i = 0;    \\n  while (true) {     \\n    yield i++; // The computation happens only on the call of next()    \\n  } \\n} \\nconst numbers = lazyNumbers(); \\nconsole.log(numbers.next().value); // 0 \\nconsole.log(numbers.next().value); // 1"],[998,"Web Workers are scripts that are carried out in the background, separated from the main thread of execution. That lets you carry out heavy computations without blocking the user interface, which improves the responsiveness of applications. Web Workers have limitations, however, such as the absence of access to the DOM and the use of a secure context only.","Web Workers are a mechanism in JavaScript that lets you carry out scripts in the background. That is especially useful for carrying out resource-intensive tasks, such as the processing of data or the carrying out of computations, without blocking the main thread, which is responsible for the user interface.\\nThe advantages of Web Workers:\\n\\n- Parallel execution: Web Workers let you carry out tasks in parallel with the main thread, improving an application's performance and responsiveness.\\n\\n- The separation of tasks: The scripts of Web Workers work in a separate context and do not block the user interface, which makes them ideal for heavy computations.\\n\\nThe limitations of Web Workers:\\n\\n- No access to the DOM: Web Workers cannot interact with the DOM directly, which limits their use for certain tasks.\\n\\n- A secure context: Web Workers work in an isolated context and can exchange messages only by means of the \`postMessage\` API.\\n\\nAn example of the creation and use of a Web Worker:\\n\\nIn this example the main thread creates a Web Worker and sends it a message. The worker handles the message and sends a response back. That lets you carry out parallel tasks without blocking the interface.","// main.js \\nconst worker = new Worker('worker.js'); \\nworker.postMessage('Hello, Worker!'); \\nworker.onmessage = function(event) {\\n  console.log('Received from worker:', event.data); \\n}; \\n// worker.js \\nonmessage = function(event) {\\n  console.log('Received from main thread:', event.data);    \\n  postMessage('Hello, Main!'); \\n};"],[999,"The synchronisation between the main thread and Web Workers is carried out through the message mechanism, using the \`postMessage\` method and \`onmessage\` handlers. The main thread sends messages into the worker, and the worker can respond in the same way. The synchronisation is not instantaneous, however, so it matters to take into account that the messages are passed asynchronously.","To synchronise the work between the main thread and Web Workers in JavaScript, an asynchronous mechanism of exchanging messages is used. The main steps include:\\n\\n- The creation of the Web Worker: You create a new worker, stating the script file that will be carried out in the background.\\n\\n- The sending of messages: The main thread can send data into the worker by means of the \`postMessage\` method, passing any data (objects, strings and so on).\\n\\n- The handling of messages: Inside the worker you can set an \`onmessage\` event handler, which will react to the messages received. The worker can handle the data and then send the results back into the main thread.\\n\\nAn example of synchronisation:\\n\\nThe synchronisation between the main thread and Web Workers is thus achieved by means of the asynchronous exchange of messages, which lets you manage tasks and results effectively. Remember that the handling of messages is asynchronous, so it matters to design the logic of the interaction between the threads correctly.","// main.js \\nconst worker = new Worker('worker.js'); \\n// We send a message into the worker \\nworker.postMessage('Start processing'); \\n// The handling of the response from the worker \\nworker.onmessage = function(event) {\\n  console.log('Result from worker:', event.data); \\n}; \\n// worker.js \\nonmessage = function(event) {\\n  console.log('Worker received:', event.data);    \\n  // The handling of the data (computations, for example)    \\n  const result = 'Processed data'; // Your computations    \\n  postMessage(result); // The sending of the result back \\n};"],[1000,"In JavaScript there are two queues for handling asynchronous tasks: the microtask queue and the task queue. The microtask queue contains tasks such as \`Promise\` and \`MutationObserver\`, which are carried out after the current execution stack has finished but before the tasks from the task queue are carried out. The task queue, by contrast, contains tasks connected with events and timers, which are handled only after every microtask has been carried out.","JavaScript uses a single-threaded model of execution, which means that at any moment in time only one thread of code is being carried out. To handle asynchronous operations such as callbacks, \`Promise\`s and events effectively, JavaScript uses two queues: the microtask queue and the task queue.\\nThe Microtask Queue:\\nContains the microtasks, which include the tasks created by means of \`Promise.then()\`, \`Promise.catch()\` and \`MutationObserver\`.\\nMicrotasks are carried out after the current operation has finished but before any tasks from the task queue are carried out. That lets higher-priority tasks be handled faster and guarantees they will be carried out before the other events.\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe result of the execution:\\n\\nThe Task Queue:\\nContains the tasks connected with events, timers and asynchronous operations. The tasks from the task queue are carried out only after every microtask has been carried out.\\nThe task queue handles the tasks in the order in which they arrive, which can lead to delays in the execution when there are a multitude of microtasks.\\nAn understanding of the differences between these two queues helps developers optimise performance and manage asynchronous code better, avoiding unexpected delays in the handling of tasks.","console.log('Start'); \\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\\n  console.log('Microtask 1'); \\n}); \\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log('Task 1'); \\n}, 0); \\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\\n  console.log('Microtask 2'); \\n}); \\nconsole.log('End');\\n\\n// Start End Microtask 1 Microtask 2 Task 1"]]`);export{e as default};